item.content:encoded:
&lt;p&gt;In 2021 a dev.to author caught a candidate by the white glow of a search page on their face. In 2026 that tell is gone. Overlay tools listen to the call, send the question to an LLM and show the answer in a window that screen sharing can't see. The candidate just reads it out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hiring teams are reacting the way you'd expect: more in-person rounds, harder follow-up questions, detection software. It's an arms race, and I don't think detection wins it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where the line actually is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The technology isn't the problem. Hiding it is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An interview tests what you know. If an AI answers for you and the interviewer doesn't know, you're misrepresenting yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sales call is different. Nobody expects a rep to remember 100 pages of product docs, pricing rules and integration details. Pulling the right fact from your own company's knowledge base while the customer is talking is just doing the job well.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Same technology, opposite ethics. So we built a real-time assistant for the second case, and we say plainly that it's not for interviews.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How it works&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The architecture is simple on paper:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A local agent captures call audio on the user's machine.&lt;br&gt;
Audio streams to Deepgram for real-time transcription.&lt;br&gt;
The transcript and the relevant part of the knowledge base go to a cloud LLM.&lt;br&gt;
A hint appears on the user's screen while the call is still going.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The knowledge base has two layers. The shared one holds company-wide material: product docs, pricing, objection handling. The personal one holds the rep's own notes. In our current setup that's around 100 pages of text per company.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why native C++ and not Electron
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A call assistant runs for hours next to Zoom or Google Meet, which already eat a lot of CPU and memory. Shipping another copy of Chromium on top of that felt wrong. We wrote the agent in C++ so it stays light, starts fast and captures audio close to the OS instead of through a browser layer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is real: more platform-specific code and slower UI iteration. For a tool that runs all day in the background, resource use mattered more to us than UI speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What we're still figuring out
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Latency. The hint has to arrive before the moment in the conversation passes. Every step in the chain adds delay.&lt;br&gt;
Relevance. A wrong hint is worse than no hint. Retrieval quality matters more than model size.&lt;br&gt;
Disclosure. Should the other side of the call know the rep has an assistant? We have opinions, but I'd like to hear yours.&lt;br&gt;
The point&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If AI can answer anything, the useful question isn't how to catch people using it. It's where using it is honest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're working on something similar, or think we drew the line in the wrong place, I'd like to hear about it in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: I'm building &lt;a href="https://realtimer.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;realtimer.ai&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;YouTube thumbnails play an important role in attracting viewers. Whether you're a content creator, web developer, designer, or someone researching video content, you may sometimes need to download a thumbnail for reference or analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The good news is that you don't always need complicated software to do it. You can retrieve a YouTube video's thumbnail using its video ID and a publicly accessible image URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this article, we'll explore how YouTube thumbnail URLs work and how you can download thumbnails in a few simple steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What Is a YouTube Thumbnail?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A YouTube thumbnail is the preview image displayed alongside a video. It gives viewers a quick idea of what the video is about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Creators use thumbnails to communicate a video's topic, establish visual identity, and encourage people to click.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For developers, these images can also be useful when building video directories, media dashboards, or other projects that display YouTube content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Step 1: Find the YouTube Video ID&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every standard YouTube video URL contains a unique video ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, consider this URL:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"&lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ&lt;/a&gt;"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The video ID is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"dQw4w9WgXcQ"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You'll use this ID to construct the thumbnail image URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Step 2: Use the Thumbnail URL&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;YouTube thumbnails are often accessible through URLs following this pattern:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"&lt;a href="https://img.youtube.com/vi/VIDEO_ID/maxresdefault.jpg" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://img.youtube.com/vi/VIDEO_ID/maxresdefault.jpg&lt;/a&gt;"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Replace "VIDEO_ID" with the actual video ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"&lt;a href="https://img.youtube.com/vi/dQw4w9WgXcQ/maxresdefault.jpg" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://img.youtube.com/vi/dQw4w9WgXcQ/maxresdefault.jpg&lt;/a&gt;"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Other commonly used thumbnail paths include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;"maxresdefault.jpg" — maximum-resolution thumbnail, when available.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;"hqdefault.jpg" — high-quality thumbnail.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;"mqdefault.jpg" — medium-quality thumbnail.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;"default.jpg" — default thumbnail.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Important: Not every video has every resolution available. If the maximum-resolution image is missing, try another available thumbnail variant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Step 3: Download the Thumbnail&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once you've constructed the URL:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open the thumbnail URL in your browser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Wait for the image to load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use your browser's image-saving option to save it to your device.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's it! You can now use the image for permitted purposes, such as personal reference or authorized design work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An Easier Method: Use a YouTube Thumbnail Downloader&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you don't want to find video IDs or construct URLs manually, an online thumbnail downloader can simplify the process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With "Toolbantu" (&lt;a href="https://toolbantu.com/tool-yt-thumbnail" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://toolbantu.com/tool-yt-thumbnail&lt;/a&gt;), you can access a dedicated YouTube Thumbnail Downloader. Copy the video link, paste it into the tool, and follow the available download options.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach is convenient for users who want a straightforward process without manually constructing image URLs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How Developers Can Build a Basic Thumbnail Preview&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're building a web application, you can create a basic thumbnail preview using JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function getYouTubeThumbnail(videoId) {&lt;br&gt;
return &lt;code&gt;https://img.youtube.com/vi/${videoId}/hqdefault.jpg&lt;/code&gt;;&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;const videoId = "dQw4w9WgXcQ";&lt;br&gt;
const thumbnailUrl = getYouTubeThumbnail(videoId);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;console.log(thumbnailUrl);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can then display the thumbnail in HTML:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
src="https://img.youtube.com/vi/dQw4w9WgXcQ/hqdefault.jpg"&lt;br&gt;
alt="YouTube video thumbnail"&lt;br&gt;
/&amp;gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This example generates a thumbnail URL and displays the image. It doesn't implement video URL validation, automatic resolution fallback, or a download button, but it provides a starting point for a larger application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Is It Legal to Download YouTube Thumbnails?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ability to access an image doesn't automatically grant permission to reuse it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thumbnails may be protected by copyright. If you plan to use one publicly, commercially, or in your own content, check the relevant permissions and applicable terms. When possible, use your own thumbnails or obtain permission from the copyright holder.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also, avoid presenting someone else's thumbnail as your own original work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Final Thoughts&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Downloading a YouTube thumbnail doesn't have to be complicated. You can retrieve an image using the video's ID and a thumbnail URL, or use an online tool to make the process easier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For developers, understanding this simple URL pattern is also useful when building applications that work with YouTube video content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want a simpler way to get started, try the "Toolbantu YouTube Thumbnail Downloader" (&lt;a href="https://toolbantu.com/tool-yt-thumbnail" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://toolbantu.com/tool-yt-thumbnail&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What approach do you prefer: constructing thumbnail URLs manually or using an online tool?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is proved, what changed in 2026, and what is still open about simultaneous approximation by a product of distances.&lt;/strong&gt; This is an exposition with complete proofs of the elementary reductions (the facts that leave only badly approximable, linearly independent pairs) and of the step that turns a counterexample into a bounded orbit, plus a small, tested Python companion (&lt;code&gt;littlewood.py&lt;/code&gt;) that computes certified continued fractions and certified Littlewood products.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By MV2 of Munim, Inc. This article and the code were researched and written with AI agents working in MV2's name. Every statement below is either proved here or cited to a source. Section 10 separates what is proved from what is open, and section 11 lists what is new here. Corrections are welcome by issue or pull request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Version 1.0, 2026-10-14. Text CC BY 4.0, code Apache-2.0.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Related problem records: Justin Sun Prize JSP-000016 (MathDB #316099, "Littlewood conjecture"), Wikipedia "Littlewood conjecture".&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


1. The question
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write ‖t‖ for the distance from a real number t to the nearest integer. &lt;strong&gt;Littlewood's conjecture&lt;/strong&gt; (posed by J. E. Littlewood around 1930) says:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For all real numbers α and β,  liminf_{q→∞} q · ‖qα‖ · ‖qβ‖ = 0.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Dirichlet's theorem gives, for one number, infinitely many q with q‖qα‖ &amp;lt; 1, and for two numbers infinitely many q with ‖qα‖, ‖qβ‖ &amp;lt; q^{−1/2}. Both give a product q‖qα‖‖qβ‖ that is merely bounded. The conjecture asks for more: the &lt;strong&gt;product&lt;/strong&gt; of the two errors, scaled by q, can be made as small as we like. Two equivalent forms are used below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The infimum form.&lt;/strong&gt; Put m(α, β) = inf_{q ≥ 1} q‖qα‖‖qβ‖. The conjecture is equivalent to m(α, β) = 0 for all pairs (Lemma 1), i.e. to m_LC = sup_{α,β} m(α, β) being 0.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Products of linear forms.&lt;/strong&gt; m(α, β) = inf |x (xα − y)(xβ − z)| over integers x ≠ 0, y, z (take y, z nearest to xα, xβ). So the conjecture asks whether this product of three linear forms in (x, y, z) takes values arbitrarily close to 0 at integer points with x ≠ 0.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Elementary reductions, with proofs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lemma 1 (infimum versus liminf).&lt;/strong&gt; liminf q‖qα‖‖qβ‖ = 0 if and only if m(α, β) = 0. &lt;em&gt;Proof.&lt;/em&gt; One direction is clear. Conversely, if some term is 0 then α or β is rational, and then infinitely many terms are 0 (multiples of the denominator). If no term is 0, all terms are positive, so an infimum of 0 is not attained and is approached by infinitely many q. ∎&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Continued fractions.&lt;/strong&gt; Let α = [a₀; a₁, a₂, ...] be irrational, with convergents pₖ/qₖ (q₋₁ = 0, q₀ = 1, qₖ₊₁ = aₖ₊₁qₖ + qₖ₋₁). Writing α = [a₀; ..., aₖ, xₖ₊₁] with complete quotient xₖ₊₁ ∈ (aₖ₊₁, aₖ₊₁ + 1), the identity qₖα − pₖ = ±1/(xₖ₊₁qₖ + qₖ₋₁) gives, for k ≥ 1,&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tex"&gt;&lt;code&gt;1 / (qₖ₊₁ + qₖ)  &amp;lt;  ‖qₖα‖  &amp;lt;  1 / qₖ₊₁.                    (Legendre's bounds)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;α is &lt;strong&gt;badly approximable&lt;/strong&gt; (α ∈ &lt;strong&gt;Bad&lt;/strong&gt;) if inf_q q‖qα‖ &amp;gt; 0. By the bounds, qₖ‖qₖα‖ &amp;lt; qₖ/qₖ₊₁ &amp;lt; 1/aₖ₊₁, so α ∉ Bad when the partial quotients are unbounded. (Conversely, bounded partial quotients imply α ∈ Bad, because the best approximations are convergents; this is classical.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lemma 2 (one coordinate suffices).&lt;/strong&gt; If α is rational or α ∉ Bad, the conjecture holds for (α, β) for every β. &lt;em&gt;Proof.&lt;/em&gt; Rational α: shown in Lemma 1. Otherwise inf_q q‖qα‖ = 0 and ‖qβ‖ ≤ 1/2, so m(α, β) ≤ ½ inf_q q‖qα‖ = 0; apply Lemma 1. ∎&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lemma 3 (linear dependence, with an explicit rate).&lt;/strong&gt; If a + bα + cβ = 0 for integers a, b, c not all zero, the conjecture holds for (α, β). More precisely, if c ≠ 0 and α is irrational, then for every convergent denominator qₖ of α (k ≥ 1) the integer n = |c|qₖ satisfies&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;n ‖nα‖ ‖nβ‖  ≤  c² |b| qₖ / qₖ₊₁²  &amp;lt;  c² |b| / qₖ₊₁.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Proof.&lt;/em&gt; If c = 0 then α is rational (b ≠ 0), and Lemma 2 applies; if α is rational, likewise. Otherwise ‖nα‖ = ‖|c|·qₖα‖ ≤ |c|·‖qₖα‖ &amp;lt; |c|/qₖ₊₁, using ‖mt‖ ≤ |m|·‖t‖ for integers m. And nβ = ±qₖ(cβ) = ∓qₖ(a + bα), so ‖nβ‖ = ‖qₖbα‖ ≤ |b|·‖qₖα‖ &amp;lt; |b|/qₖ₊₁. Multiply by n = |c|qₖ. ∎&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The test suite checks this for β = (1 + 2√2)/3, α = √2: with qₖ = 2378, qₖ₊₁ = 5741 and n = 7134 the bound is 18 · 2378 / 5741² &amp;lt; 0.0013, and the computed value is about 0.00095.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lemma 4 (a universal bound).&lt;/strong&gt; m(α, β) &amp;lt; 1/(2√5) = 0.2236... for every pair. &lt;em&gt;Proof.&lt;/em&gt; By Hurwitz's theorem every irrational α has infinitely many q with q‖qα‖ &amp;lt; 1/√5 (Borel: one of any three consecutive convergents works); multiply by ‖qβ‖ ≤ 1/2. Rational α gives m = 0. ∎&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The real question is how small m(α, β) can be made &lt;em&gt;uniformly&lt;/em&gt;: Badziahin (2016) proved m_LC &amp;lt; 1/19 and Matala-Aho, Törmä and Vapa (2026) proved m_LC &amp;lt; 1/26 (section 7). A proof that m_LC = 0 is the conjecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lemma 5 (quadratic irrationals are badly approximable).&lt;/strong&gt; If α is a real root of an irreducible f(x) = Ax² + Bx + C ∈ ℤ[x], then α ∈ Bad. &lt;em&gt;Proof.&lt;/em&gt; For integers p, q ≥ 1, q²f(p/q) is a nonzero integer, so |f(p/q)| ≥ 1/q². If |α − p/q| ≤ 1, then |f(p/q)| = |A|·|p/q − α|·|p/q − α'| ≤ |A|(|α − α'| + 1)·|α − p/q|. Hence q²|α − p/q| ≥ 1/(|A|(|α − α'| + 1)) for all p/q close to α, and q‖qα‖ is bounded below. ∎&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where this leaves the problem.&lt;/strong&gt; A counterexample must be a pair of badly approximable numbers α, β with 1, α, β linearly independent over ℚ. &lt;strong&gt;(√2, √3)&lt;/strong&gt; is such a pair (Lemma 5; 1, √2, √3 are independent), and the conjecture is &lt;strong&gt;not known&lt;/strong&gt; for it (Gowers 2009; we found no later result settling it). By Jarník, Bad has Hausdorff dimension 1, so these reductions alone leave a set of dimension 2.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Almost every pair, and the algebraic pairs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Almost every pair.&lt;/strong&gt; Bad has Lebesgue measure 0 (Khintchine 1924; Borel–Bernstein), so by Lemma 2 the possible counterexamples form a null set. Gallagher (1962) proved much more: for almost every (α, β), liminf q (log q)² ‖qα‖‖qβ‖ = 0. Chow and Yang (Invent. Math. 2024) proved Gallagher-type statements on planar lines, using effective equidistribution.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Same cubic field.&lt;/strong&gt; Cassels and Swinnerton-Dyer (1955) proved the conjecture when 1, α, β span a real cubic field, by proving the dual statement inf_{m,n≠0} |mn|·‖mα + nβ‖ = 0 and transferring it. Peck (1961) sharpened this: for a basis 1, α₁, ..., α_d of a real number field of degree d + 1, liminf q log q ‖qα₁‖...‖qα_d‖ &amp;lt; ∞. In September 2026 Dhanda, Flynn and Haynes gave a geometric proof of Peck's theorem from one application of Minkowski's convex body theorem (arXiv 2609.29469), and Bugeaud and de Mathan proved refinements toward Peck's conjecture on splitting the logarithmic saving unequally between coordinates (arXiv 2609.29360).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Two different quadratic fields&lt;/strong&gt;, such as (√2, √3), are not covered: they live in the quartic field ℚ(√2, √3), and Peck-type results for its natural basis 1, √2, √3, √6 involve the third number √6.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A related open problem.&lt;/strong&gt; No algebraic number of degree ≥ 3 is known to be badly approximable, and none is known not to be. The companion code certifies the first 41 partial quotients of 2^{1/3}, &lt;a href="https://dev.toOEIS%20A002945"&gt;1; 3, 1, 5, 1, 1, 4, 1, 1, 8, 1, 14, 1, 10, 2, 1, 4, 12, 2, 3, 2, 1, 3, 4, 1, 1, 2, 14, 3, 12, 1, 15, 3, 1, 4, 534, 1, 1, 5, 1, 1&lt;/a&gt;; the 534 is a famous surprise. If cubic irrationals are not badly approximable, as is widely expected, the Cassels–Swinnerton-Dyer theorem follows immediately from Lemma 2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Dynamics: why the exceptional set is small
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Attach to (α, β) the lattice&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tex"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Λ = &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; (x, xα − y, xβ − z) : x, y, z ∈ ℤ &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;  ⊂ ℝ³
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;(covolume 1, so a point of X₃ = SL(3, ℝ)/SL(3, ℤ)), and the diagonal matrices a_{s,t} = diag(e^{−s−t}, e^{s}, e^{t}) with s, t ≥ 0. They preserve the product of the three coordinates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lemma 6 (a counterexample gives a bounded orbit).&lt;/strong&gt; If m(α, β) = δ &amp;gt; 0, then every nonzero vector of every lattice a_{s,t}Λ (s, t ≥ 0) has sup-norm at least min(1, δ^{1/3}). By Mahler's compactness criterion the orbit {a_{s,t}Λ : s, t ≥ 0} is then bounded in X₃. &lt;em&gt;Proof.&lt;/em&gt; Take a nonzero v = a_{s,t}(x, xα − y, xβ − z). If x ≠ 0, the product of the coordinates of v is x(xα − y)(xβ − z), of absolute value ≥ δ, so its largest coordinate is ≥ δ^{1/3}. If x = 0, then v = (0, −e^{s}y, −e^{t}z) with (y, z) ≠ (0, 0) and s, t ≥ 0, so ‖v‖ ≥ 1. ∎&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the conjecture would follow from: &lt;em&gt;no such orbit is bounded&lt;/em&gt;. This is the case d = 3 of conjectures of Cassels–Swinnerton-Dyer (products of d ≥ 3 linear forms) and Margulis (bounded orbits of the full diagonal group are compact). Maucourant (2010) showed that the analogous orbit-closure statement fails for diagonal subgroups of SL(d, ℝ) with d ≥ 6; this does not affect Littlewood.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Einsiedler, Katok and Lindenstrauss (Annals 2006)&lt;/strong&gt; classified the invariant measures of positive entropy for the diagonal group on X₃ and deduced: &lt;strong&gt;the set of pairs (α, β) for which the conjecture fails has Hausdorff dimension 0&lt;/strong&gt;; in fact it is a countable union of compact sets of box dimension 0. This is still the strongest general result (Venkatesh's 2008 survey explains the proof). It does not show the set is empty. Einsiedler and Kleinbock (2007) proved the same dimension statement for the p-adic version (section 8).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Explicit pairs and limits of strengthening
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pollington–Velani (Acta Math. 2000):&lt;/strong&gt; for every α ∈ Bad, there is a set of β ∈ Bad of Hausdorff dimension 1 with q log q ‖qα‖‖qβ‖ ≤ 1 for infinitely many q.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;de Mathan (2003)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Adamczewski–Bugeaud (2006):&lt;/strong&gt; explicit pairs of badly approximable numbers, with 1, α, β independent, that satisfy the conjecture; for each α with bounded partial quotients, Adamczewski and Bugeaud construct uncountably many such β.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How much can be gained over Littlewood?&lt;/strong&gt; Gallagher's (log q)² holds almost everywhere, but Bugeaud and Moshchevitin (2011) showed that the pairs with liminf q (log q)² ‖qα‖‖qβ‖ &amp;gt; 0 have full Hausdorff dimension, and Badziahin (2013) did the same with q log q log log q. Fregoli and Kleinbock (J. Number Theory, 2026) extended Badziahin's result to ℝ^d with (log q)^{d−1} log log q.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Inhomogeneous versions.&lt;/strong&gt; Shapira (Annals 2011): for almost every (α, β), for all shifts (γ, δ), liminf |n|‖nα − γ‖‖nβ − δ‖ = 0; this also holds for pairs from totally real cubic fields. Further results on fibres by Chow–Zafeiropoulos (2021), Chow–Technau (2024) and Stefanescu (2025).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Products of three linear forms in a picture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a fixed pair, the companion records every q at which q‖qα‖‖qβ‖ reaches a new minimum (&lt;code&gt;python littlewood.py records sqrt2 sqrt3 1000000&lt;/code&gt;). A conjecture-sized question becomes concrete: does this record sequence tend to 0 for (√2, √3)? A computation can only ever show values getting small; it cannot prove the liminf is 0, and it cannot exhibit a counterexample. One value that can be checked by hand: at q = 41,&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;41√2 = 57.98275...,  41√3 = 71.01408...,   41 · 0.0172439 · 0.0140831 = 0.0099568...
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;already below the universal bound 1/26 of section 7. (&lt;code&gt;python littlewood.py value sqrt2 sqrt3 41&lt;/code&gt; prints a certified interval of width below 10⁻³⁰.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. What has been computed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Universal bounds on m_LC.&lt;/strong&gt; Badziahin (Exp. Math. 2016) gave an algorithm that certifies inequalities m_LC &amp;lt; ε, and proved m_LC &amp;lt; 1/19 (and an analogous 2-adic bound of 1/9). Matala-Aho, Törmä and Vapa (Exp. Math., online 2026-04-02) gave a new algorithm based on the continued fractions of α and β and proved &lt;strong&gt;inf_q q‖qα‖‖qβ‖ &amp;lt; 1/26 for all real α, β&lt;/strong&gt;. These are upper bounds for the worst pair; the conjecture says the worst pair still gives 0.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;p-adic numerics.&lt;/strong&gt; Badziahin's "Estimating lower limit in the p-adic Littlewood conjecture" (arXiv 2506.16860, 2025) and Vitorino–Vucusic (Mathematika 2026) give further bounds for the 2-adic case.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


8. The 2025–2026 developments
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(a) The uniform Littlewood conjecture is false.&lt;/strong&gt; Bandi, Fregoli and Kleinbock (arXiv 2504.02258, 2025) proposed a uniform strengthening: for every pair,&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tex"&gt;&lt;code&gt;lim&lt;span class="p"&gt;_{&lt;/span&gt;Q→∞&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;  Q · min&lt;span class="p"&gt;_{&lt;/span&gt;1≤q≤Q&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; ‖qα‖‖qβ‖ = 0,
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;which they knew to hold for almost every pair. Since q ≤ Q, this implies Littlewood's conjecture; it asks that &lt;em&gt;every&lt;/em&gt; large Q has a good q ≤ Q, not just infinitely many. In 2026:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Schleischitz (arXiv 2603.12611, March) &lt;strong&gt;disproved&lt;/strong&gt; it: the counterexamples form a residual set (a dense G_δ). The disproof is semi-constructive, using effective results toward Zaremba's conjecture (Bourgain–Kontorovich). He also disproved a uniform p-adic version.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Moshchevitin (arXiv 2605.26188, May) gave a short proof of a counterexample using properties of Fibonacci numbers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Shulga (arXiv 2608.25059, August) proved the counterexamples with badly approximable first coordinate form a set of Hausdorff dimension at least 3/2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Neckrasov, Wu and Yang (arXiv 2608.24401, August–September) proved the set of counterexamples is hyperplane absolute winning, so it has &lt;strong&gt;full&lt;/strong&gt; Hausdorff dimension 2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; affect the classical conjecture, whose exceptional set has dimension 0 (section 4). The contrast is the point: the uniform statement fails on a big set; the classical one can fail only on a set of dimension 0. &lt;code&gt;python littlewood.py uniform X Y Q&lt;/code&gt; prints the uniform quantity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(b) Function fields: the analogy breaks in odd characteristic.&lt;/strong&gt; De Mathan and Teulié also proposed an analogue of the p-adic conjecture (part (c)) for Laurent series over a finite field 𝔽_q, with an irreducible polynomial P(t) in place of p: the t-adic and P(t)-adic Littlewood conjectures. Adiceam, Nesharim and Lunnon (Duke 2021) found an explicit counterexample over 𝔽₃. Garrett and Robertson (Math. Comp. 2026) found computer-assisted counterexamples to the P(t)-adic version over small fields (characteristic 5, and the characteristic-3 construction in characteristics 7 and 11); Robertson (Mathematika 2026) gave a transference from t to any irreducible P(t) using number walls. &lt;strong&gt;Lai and Sprang (arXiv 2606.00633, May 2026) proved that the P(t)-adic Littlewood conjecture fails for every irreducible P(t) over every ground field of odd characteristic.&lt;/strong&gt; Characteristic 2 is open. Badziahin, Pavlenkov and Zorin (arXiv 2608.22078, August 2026) showed that in the t-adic case a non-empty exceptional set has infinite 1/log(1/r)-Hausdorff measure, refining the Lai–Sprang construction for odd q. None of this implies anything about real numbers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(c) The p-adic Littlewood conjecture&lt;/strong&gt; (de Mathan–Teulié 2004): for every real α and prime p, inf_q q·|q|_p·‖qα‖ = 0. Proved for quadratic irrationals (de Mathan–Teulié); exceptional set of dimension 0 (Einsiedler–Kleinbock 2007); still open. Badziahin, Pavlenkov and Zorin (2026) showed that if the exceptional set is non-empty, it has positive Hausdorff measure for the gauge 1/log(1/r), so it has the cardinality of the continuum. &lt;code&gt;python littlewood.py mixed X P Q&lt;/code&gt; records q|q|_P‖qX‖.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


9. Status as of 2026-10-14
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Open.&lt;/strong&gt; We found no 2025–2026 result proving or disproving the classical conjecture. The most recent papers on it (Shulga, August 2026; Neckrasov–Wu–Yang, September 2026) describe it as open, with the dimension-0 theorem of Einsiedler–Katok–Lindenstrauss as the best general result; Wikipedia's article (edited 2026-10-05) agrees. The Justin Sun Prize MathDB pilot register lists JSP-000016 with no solution, Lean or exposition entry. We found no claimed proof on arXiv in 2025–2026.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What moved in 2026:&lt;/strong&gt; the uniform version was disproved (with full-dimensional counterexamples), the P(t)-adic analogue was disproved in every odd characteristic, the universal bound improved from 1/19 to 1/26, Badziahin's logarithmic obstruction was extended to higher dimensions, and Peck's theorem got a geometric proof and refinements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


10. Proved versus open, as of 2026-10-14
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Proved:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The conjecture holds when α or β is rational or not badly approximable, and when 1, α, β are linearly dependent over ℚ (Lemmas 2 and 3).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;m(α, β) &amp;lt; 1/26 for every pair (Matala-Aho–Törmä–Vapa 2026; earlier 1/19, Badziahin 2016; 1/(2√5) by Hurwitz, Lemma 4).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It holds for almost every pair, with the factor (log q)² (Gallagher 1962), and for pairs spanning a real cubic field with 1, with the factor log q (Cassels–Swinnerton-Dyer 1955; Peck 1961).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The exceptional set has Hausdorff dimension 0 (Einsiedler–Katok–Lindenstrauss 2006); same for the p-adic version (Einsiedler–Kleinbock 2007).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For each α ∈ Bad, a dimension-1 set of β ∈ Bad satisfies it (Pollington–Velani 2000); explicit independent badly approximable pairs satisfy it (de Mathan 2003; Adamczewski–Bugeaud 2006).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The uniform Littlewood conjecture is false, with counterexamples of full dimension (2026).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The P(t)-adic analogue is false over every field of odd characteristic (Lai–Sprang 2026, building on Adiceam–Nesharim–Lunnon 2021 and Garrett–Robertson 2026).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Open:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Littlewood's conjecture itself: is m(α, β) = 0 for every pair? Equivalently, is m_LC = 0?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Any specific independent pair of algebraic numbers from different quadratic fields, starting with (√2, √3).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The p-adic Littlewood conjecture (de Mathan–Teulié), and the t-adic one in characteristic 2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Cassels–Swinnerton-Dyer and Margulis conjectures on diagonal orbits in X₃.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whether any algebraic number of degree ≥ 3 is badly approximable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


11. What is original here
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Self-contained proofs of the reductions (Lemmas 1–5), including an explicit rate c²|b|qₖ/qₖ₊₁² in the linearly dependent case, and of the step from a counterexample to a bounded diagonal orbit (Lemma 6).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;certified continued fraction&lt;/strong&gt; method from an exact floor oracle: if N·x is available as ⌊N x⌋ in integers, the reals whose floor algorithm starts with a₀, ..., a_{j−1} form an interval, so the terms shared by the expansions of ⌊2ᵇx⌋/2ᵇ and (⌊2ᵇx⌋ + 1)/2ᵇ are proved terms of x. With integer k-th roots this certifies, for example, 41 partial quotients of 2^{1/3}, including the 534.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Certified Littlewood products&lt;/strong&gt;: since ‖·‖ is 1-Lipschitz and qx lies in [qA/S, (qA + q)/S) for A = ⌊Sx⌋, each value q‖qα‖‖qβ‖ comes with a proved interval; with S = 2²⁵⁶ the width is far below 10⁻³⁰ for q in the millions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;littlewood.py&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;test_littlewood.py&lt;/code&gt;: exact reals ((a + b√d)/c and (p/q)^{1/k}), certified continued fractions and convergents, certified distances, record scans of q‖qα‖‖qβ‖, the uniform quantity of section 8(a), and the p-adic quantity of section 8(c), in plain Python with no dependencies. The tests check Legendre's bounds and Borel's theorem on convergents, Lemma 3 on a dependent pair, the value at q = 41 for (√2, √3), and the cube root of 2 against OEIS A002945.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing here claims progress on the conjecture itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


12. Using the companion code
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python littlewood.py cf cbrt2 41                 &lt;span class="c"&gt;# certified partial quotients of 2^(1/3)&lt;/span&gt;
python littlewood.py records sqrt2 sqrt3 100000  &lt;span class="c"&gt;# new minima of q ||q sqrt2|| ||q sqrt3||&lt;/span&gt;
python littlewood.py value sqrt2 sqrt3 41        &lt;span class="c"&gt;# certified interval for one value&lt;/span&gt;
python littlewood.py uniform sqrt2 sqrt3 100000  &lt;span class="c"&gt;# T * min_{q&amp;lt;=T} ||q x|| ||q y||, T = 1, 2, 4, ...&lt;/span&gt;
python littlewood.py mixed sqrt2 2 100000        &lt;span class="c"&gt;# new minima of q |q|_2 ||q sqrt2||&lt;/span&gt;
python &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-m&lt;/span&gt; unittest test_littlewood               &lt;span class="c"&gt;# or: python test_littlewood.py&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Numbers: &lt;code&gt;sqrt2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sqrt(7)&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;cbrt2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;2^1/3&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;2^2/3&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;golden&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;surd:a,b,d,c&lt;/code&gt; for (a + b√d)/c. Python 3.6+, no dependencies.&lt;/p&gt;

