&lt;h2&gt;


Background
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Many organizations keep data in Salesforce and run their applications on AWS. Keeping the two in sync often goes through an integration platform such as Boomi or MuleSoft, which adds licensing cost and another system to run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This article shows how to use &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_intro.htm&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Salesforce Event Relay&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-saas-salesforce.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon EventBridge partner integration for Salesforce&lt;/a&gt; to synchronize data from Salesforce to AWS through EventBridge, without middleware.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the example, when a customer is created in Salesforce, the customer details are sent to EventBridge and stored in DynamoDB for the AWS applications to use. The full code, including the Salesforce metadata and the CDK stack for the AWS side, is in the &lt;a href="https://github.com/ssathasivan/salesforce-event-relay-eventbridge" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub repo&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Architecture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhfpxgi2p7oana2diy4se.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhfpxgi2p7oana2diy4se.png" alt="Architecture" width="800" height="267"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Salesforce
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a record is created, updated, or deleted, Salesforce can publish a &lt;a href="https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/platform/platform-events/guide/platform-events-intro.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;platform event&lt;/a&gt;. A platform event is a message that systems outside Salesforce can subscribe to. Salesforce doesn't publish it on its own: a record-triggered Flow or an Apex trigger publishes the event when the record changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Published events go to the Salesforce &lt;strong&gt;event bus&lt;/strong&gt;. Subscribers read events from the bus. The bus &lt;a href="https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/platform/pub-sub-api/guide/event-message-durability.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;keeps events for 72 hours&lt;/a&gt;, which makes replay possible in case of an outage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this example, when a customer is created, Salesforce publishes a platform event called &lt;code&gt;Customer_Created__e&lt;/code&gt; with these fields:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Type&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Description&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;Account_Id__c&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Text(18)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Salesforce ID of the Account (customer) record&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;Name__c&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Text(255)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Customer name&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;Industry__c&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Text(40)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Customer industry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;CreatedDate&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Date/Time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Standard field, set by Salesforce when the event is published&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;CreatedById&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Lookup(User)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Standard field, the user who published the event&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Salesforce Event Relay
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_intro.htm&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Event Relay&lt;/a&gt; is a Salesforce feature that reads events from the Salesforce event bus and delivers them to Amazon EventBridge in your AWS account. Salesforce keeps track of the last event it delivered. After a transient error, such as a connectivity issue, the relay resumes from where it left off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A relay is built from three Salesforce components:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Named credential.&lt;/strong&gt; It tells Salesforce where to deliver: your AWS account ID and region, written as a URL such as &lt;code&gt;arn:aws:US-EAST-1:123456789012&lt;/code&gt;. It contains no AWS keys and no IAM role. Salesforce is a registered EventBridge partner, so &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_security.htm&amp;amp;language=en_US&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;no AWS authentication is involved&lt;/a&gt;. The region must be uppercase. Once a relay uses the credential, you &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_create_named_credential.htm&amp;amp;language=en_US&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;can't change the account or region&lt;/a&gt; without deleting and recreating the relay.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Channel.&lt;/strong&gt; It decides what to deliver. A channel groups one or more event types, and each event type is added as a channel member. In this example the channel has one member, &lt;code&gt;Customer_Created__e&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Event relay.&lt;/strong&gt; It connects one channel to one named credential, and it holds the recovery setting described in Recovery after an outage. A new relay starts out &lt;a href="https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.api_meta.meta/api_meta/meta_eventrelayconfig.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;stopped&lt;/a&gt;. After it's created, you can change only its state and recovery setting. A wrong channel or credential means deleting the relay and creating it again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you create the relay, Salesforce registers a &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_create_ui.htm&amp;amp;language=en_US&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;partner event source&lt;/a&gt; in your AWS account, in a pending state. You associate that source with an event bus in AWS, then start the relay in Salesforce. Events start flowing at that point. That bus is a partner event bus, and it receives only the events from this relay. One relay maps to one channel, one partner event source, and one partner bus.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To configure each piece, follow Salesforce's guide &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_events_section.htm&amp;amp;language=en_US&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Relay Events from Salesforce to Amazon EventBridge&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What arrives in AWS
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a relayed event as EventBridge delivers it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"version"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"6f33d717-8e35-4488-5690-89e681b5737c"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"detail-type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Customer_Created__e"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"source"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"aws.partner/salesforce.com/00D.../0YL..."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"account"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"123456789012"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-10-13T19:23:23Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"region"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"us-east-1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"resources"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"detail"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"payload"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"CreatedById"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"005..."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"CreatedDate"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-10-13T19:23:22.686Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Account_Id__c"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"0015g00000AbCdEAAV"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Name__c"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Example Corp"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"Industry__c"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Manufacturing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"schemaId"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"mVBhpA7tU9MCtBEbqjycBQ"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"118dab75-7b45-498e-8478-52d0324c1060"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;source&lt;/code&gt; contains the Salesforce org ID and channel ID.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;detail-type&lt;/code&gt; is the Salesforce event name, which is what the rule matches on.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The fields are under &lt;code&gt;detail.payload&lt;/code&gt;, with their Salesforce API names.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The top-level &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt; comes from EventBridge. &lt;code&gt;detail.id&lt;/code&gt; comes from Salesforce.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


EventBridge rule
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule matches &lt;code&gt;detail-type&lt;/code&gt; = &lt;code&gt;Customer_Created__e&lt;/code&gt; and sends those events to the SQS queue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Amazon SQS queue and DLQ
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule sends events to an SQS queue instead of invoking the Lambda directly, with a DLQ for messages that can't be processed. The queue absorbs bursts, lets you cap Lambda concurrency so DynamoDB isn't overloaded, and retries failed messages. Messages that keep failing stay in the DLQ, so you can redrive them after a fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Lambda and DynamoDB
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Lambda reads the customer details from the event and writes them to DynamoDB. That is where the AWS applications read the data from.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Design decisions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Recovery after an outage
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the relay hits an error it can't resume from, a recovery setting decides what happens (&lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_error_recovery.htm&amp;amp;language=en_US&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Error Recovery&lt;/a&gt;):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;LATEST&lt;/code&gt; (the default) skips events published during the outage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;EARLIEST&lt;/code&gt; re-sends everything still retained on the Salesforce event bus, up to 72 hours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For business data, &lt;code&gt;EARLIEST&lt;/code&gt; is usually the right choice, because a missed event means a customer the AWS applications don't know about. The cost is duplicates. Between &lt;code&gt;EARLIEST&lt;/code&gt; recovery, EventBridge retries, and SQS retries, the same event can be delivered more than once, so the application must be designed to process duplicate messages. In this example, the Lambda writes with a conditional put keyed on the Account ID and treats an existing record as success, so duplicates never reach the DLQ.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Small payloads
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Salesforce &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_feature_support.htm&amp;amp;language=en&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documents a 256 KB limit&lt;/a&gt; for relayed events. Larger events aren't delivered to EventBridge. Hence, the event should carry only the record ID and the fields the AWS applications need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Monitoring the relay
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the relay goes into an error state, delivery stops until Salesforce recovers. It retries periodically and resumes from where it left off, but it doesn't notify anyone (&lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_status.htm&amp;amp;language=en_US&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Relay status&lt;/a&gt;). Monitor the relay by querying &lt;code&gt;EventRelayFeedback&lt;/code&gt;, a Salesforce object that holds the relay's status and error details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Cost
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the Salesforce side, &lt;a href="https://developer.salesforce.com/blogs/2022/10/salesforce-event-relay-is-generally-available" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Event Relay is included at no extra cost&lt;/a&gt;. Relayed events count toward the org's daily event delivery allocation. By default, that's &lt;a href="https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/platform/platform-events/guide/platform-events-limits-entitlement.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;50,000 events a day for Unlimited and Performance editions and 25,000 for Enterprise Edition&lt;/a&gt;. If you need more, Salesforce sells a &lt;a href="https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/platform/platform-events/guide/platform-events-limits-entitlement.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;platform events add-on&lt;/a&gt; that raises it by 100,000 events a day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the AWS side, events arriving on the partner event bus are billed as &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/pricing/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;partner events&lt;/a&gt;, at $1.00 per million events. Each 64 KB chunk of an event counts as one event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Salesforce edition.&lt;/strong&gt; Event Relay is &lt;a href="https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/platform/event-relay/guide/intro.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;available in Enterprise, Unlimited, and Developer editions&lt;/a&gt;. It isn't available in Government Cloud. A free Developer Edition org is enough to build and test this example.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Event types.&lt;/strong&gt; Event Relay works with &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_feature_support.htm&amp;amp;language=en&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;custom high-volume platform events&lt;/a&gt;, which is the default for any new custom platform event such as &lt;code&gt;Customer_Created__e&lt;/code&gt;. It also works with Change Data Capture and Real-Time Event Monitoring events.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AWS region.&lt;/strong&gt; Event Relay can &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_considerations.htm&amp;amp;language=en_US&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;deliver only to default AWS regions&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/manage-acct-regions.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Default regions&lt;/a&gt;, such as &lt;code&gt;us-east-1&lt;/code&gt;, are enabled in every AWS account. &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/manage-acct-regions.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Opt-in regions&lt;/a&gt;, such as Europe (Milan), have to be enabled first, and Event Relay doesn't support them.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Delivery allocation.&lt;/strong&gt; Salesforce limits how many events an org can deliver in 24 hours, and every event the relay sends to EventBridge &lt;a href="https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.ev_relay_allocations.htm&amp;amp;language=en_US&amp;amp;type=5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;counts toward that limit&lt;/a&gt;. Other integrations that subscribe to events in the same org share it, so relay only the events AWS needs.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Last quarter, three of us sat down to debug why an AI agent kept hitting a 404 on an endpoint we'd renamed the week before. We opened Bruno, searched for the old path, found nothing, and then we sat quiet for a second. The rename had happened in our OpenAPI spec. None of us had re-generated the collection. Bruno had no idea it was out of sync, and neither did the agent that was reading the collection as its map of the API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That moment is the subject of this post. Bruno is, genuinely, one of the best things to happen to API tooling in years. The cloud-first model was a trap — your collections lived on someone else's server, your team's access was gated on a subscription, and diffing a request across branches meant opening two browser tabs. Bruno killed that with a Git-native, file-on-disk, no-lock-in model that feels like using a code editor, not a SaaS console. If you haven't used it, you should.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What Bruno did not solve is the drift problem. It solved &lt;em&gt;where&lt;/em&gt; your requests live. It did not solve &lt;em&gt;what they are true against&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The drift that a human tolerates, and an agent cannot
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Bruno, a collection is a set of &lt;code&gt;.bru&lt;/code&gt; files. You can import an OpenAPI spec once, and you can hand-write requests, and you can commit everything to Git. That works beautifully when a human is the one clicking through: you change a path, you notice the collection still has the old one, you fix it in five minutes, you move on. Drift is a tax you pay when you remember.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An AI agent does not remember. An agent that is given a Bruno collection as its view of the API will call the old path, get a 404, and either retry with variations it invents (now you're watching it probe your live endpoint) or give up and report that the endpoint doesn't exist. It will not, on its own, re-read the spec file on disk and notice that the path moved. The collection was presented as the map, so the collection is the map.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the gap that showed up for us. Bruno's import is a snapshot. There is no step that says, before you call anything, re-run the spec against the live endpoint and tell me what moved. There is no diff between what the spec promises and what the server actually returns. The tool assumes a human will look at the screen and feel that something is off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Local-first was never the endgame. Re-run was.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The local-first bet was correct and important. Cloud lock-in for developer tools is real, and Postman's pricing over the years made the case for it. But once you accept that the files should live on your disk and in your repo, the next question is: what does the tool &lt;em&gt;do&lt;/em&gt; with those files? Most local-first API tools treat them as a place to store requests you've already written. We started treating them as something to re-run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The difference is small in words and large in practice. A tool that stores requests lets you replay them. A tool that re-runs the spec tells you, before you call anything, that the &lt;code&gt;GET /v2/widgets&lt;/code&gt; the spec describes returns a schema the live endpoint no longer matches, that the auth header the collection assumes was renamed in the spec, that the parameter you marked as optional is now required. For a human, that's a useful heads-up. For an agent, that's the difference between a task that finishes and a task that spends ten minutes guessing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built &lt;a href="https://www.powerduck.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Powerduck&lt;/a&gt; around this. It's local-first in the same bet as Bruno — your spec and your state live on disk, in Git, no tenant in the middle — but the unit of work is not a request you replay, it's the OpenAPI spec you re-run against your live endpoint. The agent gets the drift report, the diff, and the regenerated paths back, instead of a stale collection to hallucinate against.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What this means for choosing a tool in 2026
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're a solo developer or a small team clicking through requests by hand, Bruno is still the right call and I wouldn't argue with you. The local-first, Git-native, no-subscription model is a real step up, and the ecosystem around it is good. The question changes the moment an agent is in the loop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once an AI tool is reading your API as a map, you need that map to be re-derivable from the source of truth, not hand-maintained. The source of truth for an API is the OpenAPI spec, not a collection someone imported last quarter. Any tool — local or cloud — that stores requests without re-running the spec is going to drift, and the drift is going to land on your agent's error messages, not your human's.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the framing I'd use when you're evaluating tools now is not "cloud vs local." That fight is over; local won, and Bruno won it. The next fight is "does your tool re-run the spec, or just store your requests." The answer to that question is what decides whether an agent can trust the tool, or whether you'll be sitting with your team at 4 PM wondering why it's still calling the old path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Automating Multi‑Platform Content Publishing with a Tiny Node Script
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I built a Node / TypeScript script that pulls the latest changelog, renders platform‑specific markdown, updates &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;, and commits the files in one go. This removes manual copy‑paste, keeps platform flags in sync, and lets CI skip builds for pure‑content commits.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our weekly sprint ends with a handful of blog‑style updates (Medium, Substack, Bluesky) and a changelog for internal tracking. Previously I was:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Manually copying the same text into several &lt;code&gt;*.md&lt;/code&gt; files.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Forgetting to flip the &lt;code&gt;"medium_generated"&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;"substack_generated"&lt;/code&gt; flags in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Accidentally triggering a full CI pipeline on a commit that only touched content, wasting minutes on a cold Render instance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The symptom was a &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;skip ci&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; comment added after the fact and a growing backlog of mismatched metadata. The error message from the CI logs was simply “No build steps to run”, but the real pain was the manual overhead and the risk of publishing incomplete or out‑of‑sync posts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first attempt was a &lt;strong&gt;shell script&lt;/strong&gt; that &lt;code&gt;cat&lt;/code&gt;‑ed a template file into each platform folder:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;#!/bin/bash&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cp &lt;/span&gt;templates/weekly.md content/&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt; +%Y/%m/%d&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;/VS/medium_en.md
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cp &lt;/span&gt;templates/weekly.md content/&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt; +%Y/%m/%d&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;/VS/substack_en.md
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I added &lt;code&gt;git commit -m "chore(content): auto‑generate $(date)"&lt;/code&gt; afterwards.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What went wrong?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No validation of the target directories – the script crashed when the date‑based folder didn’t exist.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; flag updates were missing, so the downstream publishing bots thought the content wasn’t ready.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The script was not part of the repo, so each team member had a slightly different version, leading to subtle formatting differences.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Directory layout
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;content/
└─ 2026/
└─ 10/
└─ 13/
├─ VS/
│  ├─ changelog.md
│  ├─ medium_en.md
│  ├─ medium_es.md
│  ├─ substack_en.md
│  ├─ substack_es.md
│  ├─ bluesky_es.json
│  └─ metadata.json
└─ content-automation/
└─ … (same structure for other projects)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;All platform‑specific files live under the same date folder, making it easy for a single script to discover the target path with &lt;code&gt;path.join&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Core script (&lt;code&gt;scripts/generate-content.ts&lt;/code&gt;)
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;promises&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// -------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Helpers&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// -------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ensureDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mkdir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;recursive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Render a simple markdown template with injected variables&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderTemplate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;template&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;template&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\{\{(\w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;)\}\}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// -------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Main&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// -------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;today&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;datePath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;__dirname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;..&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;today&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getFullYear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;today&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getMonth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;padStart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;today&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;padStart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;VS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ensureDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;datePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 1️⃣ Load the raw changelog (generated elsewhere)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rawChangelog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;datePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;changelog.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 2️⃣ Prepare platform‑specific bodies&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;platforms&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;medium_en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;medium_es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;substack_en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;substack_es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;bluesky_es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;template&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;__dirname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;../templates/weekly.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;platforms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rendered&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderTemplate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;template&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;LANG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;CHANGELOG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rawChangelog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;bluesky&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;.md&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;filePath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;datePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 3️⃣ Write the platform file&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;filePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rendered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`✅ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; generated`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 4️⃣ Update metadata flags&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;datePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;metadata.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaRaw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaRaw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;medium_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;substack_generated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;✅ metadata.json flags updated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 5️⃣ Commit with [skip ci] automatically&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;execSync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;child_process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;git add .&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;stdio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;inherit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`git commit -m "chore(content): auto-generate &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;today&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toISOString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; [skip ci]"`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;stdio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;inherit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;git push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;stdio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;inherit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;❌ Generation failed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;exit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key points in the code&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Line&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why it matters&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;await fs.mkdir(dir, { recursive: true })&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Guarantees the date folder exists, fixing the crash from the shell script.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;renderTemplate&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Centralises variable interpolation (&lt;code&gt;{{LANG}}&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;{{CHANGELOG}}&lt;/code&gt;) so we only maintain one source of truth.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Platform loop (&lt;code&gt;platforms&lt;/code&gt; array)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Adding a new platform is as easy as pushing a new object – no copy‑paste.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; update&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Directly toggles &lt;code&gt;"medium_generated"&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;"substack_generated"&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt;, keeping publishing bots in sync.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;git commit … [skip ci]&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Automates the CI‑skip flag, removing the manual edit step.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Example of generated files (excerpt)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/13/VS/medium_en.md&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (first 10 lines)&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
markdown
I was in the middle of a sprint when two unrelated

---

*Part of my [Build in Public](https://dev.to/zaerohell) series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.*

*Repo: `zaerohell/content-automation` · 2026-10-14*

\#playadev #buildinpublic
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://www.tddbuddy.com/blog/agent-prs-do-not-converge-under-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tddbuddy.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Related reading: &lt;a href="https://www.tddbuddy.com/blog/where-the-review-point-moved/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Where the Review Point Moved&lt;/a&gt; argued the review surface migrated upstream; this post names the specific empirical failure mode of running the old review turn on an agent PR anyway. &lt;a href="https://www.tddbuddy.com/blog/reviewing-ai-with-ai-is-not-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI Reviewing AI Is Not Review&lt;/a&gt; covered noise amplification when a second agent sits in the loop; this post covers iteration asymmetry when a human reviewer is still the one commenting.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reviewer left fourteen comments on the agent's pull request, got three rounds back with cosmetic edits, and closed the tab.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That tab closing has a number next to it now. Three 2026 MSR studies landed on the same shape from different angles. "Why Are Agentic Pull Requests Merged or Rejected?" (arxiv 2605.22534) looked at 11,048 closed agentic PRs against 9,799 human-reviewed ones and found that 38% of rejected agentic PRs died of reviewer abandonment rather than an explicit decision. "When AI Teammates Meet Code Review" (arxiv 2602.19441) measured the per-comment effect: roughly +2.7% on a human PR's merge probability, roughly -2.8% on an agent PR's. The AIDev rejection paper (arxiv 2606.13468) traced the same shape across a larger corpus. Three studies, same sign flip, same mechanism, same consequence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The industry read the first wave of papers as a model-quality problem: the agent writes code the human review turn can mostly accept, with iteration patching the small gaps. The second wave reads differently. The sign on the iteration mechanic flipped. Every comment on a human PR makes the merge more likely. Every comment on an agent PR makes it less. That is not a quality curve; it is a workflow failure. More specifically, it is a design-review workflow failure, because local iteration on an agent PR still converges. The negative correlation lives in design iteration: the comment that names an intent the diff does not honor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Numbers Land on a Workflow Question, Not a Model Question
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Same iteration mechanic, opposite outcome. The variable that moved is the author.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reviewer comment is a signal. On a human PR, the signal travels into the author's head, meets a working mental model of the change, and comes out as a diff that integrates the feedback. The signal is additive: comment one sharpens the diff, comment two sharpens it further, the merge probability climbs because each round brings the artifact closer to what the reviewer and author now jointly understand. The +2.7% per comment is the shape of that climb.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On an agent PR, the signal travels into a session whose context either dropped the original design frame or never held it in the first place. The agent reads the comment, re-plans against the local diff and whatever tests it can see, and produces a new diff. The new diff touches different lines. The intent the reviewer flagged is the same intent the new diff fails to honor, because the thing the reviewer is pointing at was never in the model being used. The second round looks like iteration from the outside. It is a fresh run of the same prompt with the review comment appended. The -2.8% per comment is the shape of that re-run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Upgrading the model does not change the sign. A better optimizer runs the retrieve-and-replay faster. The retrieval still has the same gap. The replay still produces a diff that does not integrate the reviewer's intent, because the integration mechanism assumes a mental model that persists across rounds, and the agent does not hold the kind of mental model a human author updates between rounds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The sign flipped on authorship, not on quality. The author cannot be swapped out by upgrading the model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Iteration Is Where Human PRs Converge and Agent PRs Retry
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A human reviewer's design comment triggers a reconsideration. The author re-reads the spec in their head, re-locates the boundary the reviewer named, and the diff moves. The movement is not uniform: sometimes the second round is a small edit, sometimes a near-rewrite, sometimes the author pushes back and the reviewer updates their own position. All three outcomes converge, because both sides hold a working model of the change and can update it between rounds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An agent does not hold that kind of working model. It holds a session, a context window, and a diff. The context may carry the original prompt, the test suite that constrained the first run, and the review comment the second run has to address. It does not carry the chain of design decisions a human author assembled in their head between round one and round two. There is no persistent author in that sense. There is a replay.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retry is not iteration. Iteration updates a model; retry re-executes against a slightly different prompt. The visible artifact can look similar. The method name changes. A null guard appears. A test case gets added. The thing the reviewer actually asked for (a change in what the diff is trying to be) does not appear, because the frame that would need to shift was never carried into the retry.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Round one comment: "This flow treats the gift-card refund as a payment refund.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// They are different domain events; the ledger reconciliation downstream depends on it."&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Round two: the method got renamed; the enum got a new value; the ledger call still&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// routes gift-card refunds through RefundPayment(). The reviewer's intent did not land.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Round three: a comment is added above the call site explaining the distinction.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The call still routes through RefundPayment(). The reviewer closes the tab.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IRefundService&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;RefundResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;RefundPayment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;PaymentRefundRequest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;RefundResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;RestoreGiftCardBalance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;GiftCardRestoreRequest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A human author in round two reads the comment, feels the ledger reconciliation point as a load-bearing distinction, and splits the call site. The agent in round two reads the comment, finds the closest surface edit that acknowledges the words in it, and ships the edit. One is a mental-model update. The other is a text transformation. The reviewer can feel the difference by round three.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Humans iterate. Agents retry. The empirical shape is what tells the two apart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Reviewer Abandonment Is a Structural Failure, Not Reviewer Fatigue
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;38% of rejected agent PRs die because the reviewer stopped engaging. That is not an attention-budget problem. That is a convergence problem the reviewer diagnosed and refused to spend more rounds confirming.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reviewer who abandons a human PR tends to do it after an argument: a disagreement about scope, a shift in priorities, a design the author will not change and the reviewer will not approve. There is a conversation on the way out. The reviewer who abandons an agent PR tends to do it after three rounds of non-convergence. There is no argument. There is a pattern the reviewer has started to recognize across every agent PR this quarter: the comment goes in, the diff comes back cosmetically different, the intent the comment named is still missing. By round three the reviewer stops writing comments, because the comments are not causing the thing comments are supposed to cause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Calling the reviewer lazy misreads the measurement. The reviewer is responding to a signal, and the signal is honest. The loop is not converging. More rounds will not help. The reviewer closes the tab because the reviewer has learned, across the previous twenty agent PRs, that a fourth round is noise. The 38% is the aggregate of that learning across many reviewers and many PRs. It is a diagnosis delivered in the only language a review tool records: the absence of further activity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A rational reviewer with a working heuristic produces this shape. The failure is upstream of the reviewer, in a workflow that routes design feedback through a review turn the agent cannot honor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Local Iteration on an Agent PR Still Works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every review comment names an intent. Some name a surface defect: a rename, a missing test case, a one-function refactor, a boundary adjusted by a few lines. The agent integrates these in one round. The diff updates. The PR merges. The loop that still works is the loop whose feedback does not require a model update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rough heuristic sorts the two cases at the comment layer. If the comment can be answered by editing the diff without changing the spec that governs it, the comment is local. The agent re-reads the diff, applies the edit, and produces a new diff that converges. If the comment can only be answered by changing the spec (because the reviewer is pointing at an intent the current spec does not express), the comment is design. The agent, lacking a durable model of the spec, retries against the old spec with cosmetic awareness of the comment. The diff does not converge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The aggregate -2.8% per-comment correlation averages the two cases. Local iteration pulls the number up; design iteration pulls it down harder. The sharp move is not to refuse all iteration on agent PRs. The sharp move is to route each kind of feedback to the workflow that handles it. Local comments stay on the current PR. Design comments move upstream.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treating every agent PR comment as the same kind of comment is where the sign flip bites. Separate the kinds; separate the workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Move the Design Decision Upstream of the PR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The upstream move is concrete. When the reviewer's comment amounts to "this needs a different design," the next action is not another round on the current PR. The current PR closes. The design decision commits to main as a new scenario test, a new contract on the seam the reviewer flagged, a new named domain constraint the next agent run will see. A fresh PR opens against the sharpened surface. The spec the agent must satisfy is now the thing the previous round of review was trying to produce.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Sharpened spec on main (committed before the agent's next PR):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Fact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;A_gift_card_refund_restores_balance_without_touching_the_payment_ledger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;refund&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;aRefundFor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;aGiftCardPurchase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;costing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;40&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dollars&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;refundService&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;refund&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Should&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Satisfy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;giftCardBalanceRestoredBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;40&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dollars&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;paymentLedger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Should&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;NotHaveEntriesFor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;refund&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;transactionId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The scenario names the distinction the reviewer spent three rounds trying to introduce. The agent's next PR runs against a suite that fails until the distinction is honored. The diff converges in one shot, because the thing the reviewer wanted the agent to understand is now a test the agent cannot ship past. The review conversation on the new PR is two nits and a rename.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The move puts the design decision where it can land cleanly: committed to main as a durable artifact, visible to the next session as a constraint rather than as a comment on a diff that no longer exists. The reviewer has produced a spec, not a backlog of comments. The agent has a surface to converge against, not an intent to guess from English prose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The workflow rule follows from the empirical shape. If the first round of design feedback does not converge, do not run a second. Close the PR. Sharpen the spec. Open a new PR against the new surface. Iteration on the current PR was never the artifact; the sharpened spec was.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reviewer who left fourteen comments on the agent's PR and closed the tab was reading the measurement correctly. The next move is not a fifteenth comment. The next move is a scenario test on main that the agent cannot replay past.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Iteration is a model update. Retry is not. A workflow that pretends they are the same ships rejected PRs; a workflow that routes them separately ships merged ones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've started working on a project I've wanted to experiment with for a while: building my own local AI assistant, with the eventual goal of having it help me with development work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is one slight complication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My development laptop isn't exactly an AI workstation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We're talking:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Intel Celeron N4000&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;2 cores / 2 threads&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;8 GB RAM&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Intel UHD Graphics 600&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Windows 11&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No dedicated GPU. No 32 GB or 64 GB of RAM. No monster Ryzen or Core i9 sitting under the desk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Just a little Celeron doing its best.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And somehow... it's working.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, What Do I Mean by "My Own LLM"?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not training a multi-billion-parameter foundation model from scratch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That would require a completely different level of hardware, data and infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead, I'm building my own LLM-powered assistant around an existing open-weight model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model handles inference, while I'm building the software around it: the interface, conversation handling, developer tools, file access, safety boundaries, prompts, context management and eventually things such as retrieval and tool use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction is important because the model itself is only one part of an AI application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My goal isn't:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;«Build ChatGPT from scratch.»&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's much more practical:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;«Build a local AI assistant that is actually useful to me as a developer.»&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And importantly, build it in such a way that the underlying model can eventually be replaced without rebuilding the entire application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Hardware Constraint Is Part of the Project&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most local AI discussions quickly reach hardware specifications that make my laptop quietly hide in the corner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Large models simply aren't realistic on this machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With only 8 GB of system RAM and a 2-core/2-thread Celeron N4000, every decision has to take the available resources into account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That means using:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;small models;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;quantised GGUF models;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;conservative context sizes;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;sensible thread counts;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;lightweight infrastructure;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;and plenty of benchmarking.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm currently targeting the sort of 1B–3B model range that has a realistic chance of running on the hardware.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't expect enormous token speeds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's not really the point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I can ask my own locally running assistant to explain some JavaScript, diagnose a small bug, generate a regex or inspect a controlled set of files, and it gives me a useful answer, then it has done its job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In fact, I think getting useful local AI inference running on this hardware is an interesting engineering problem in itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of throwing more compute at the problem, I have to ask:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How much useful AI can I squeeze out of the computer I already have?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Getting the First Local Response&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project uses "llama.cpp" as the local inference runtime.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the first major milestones was simply getting from:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My laptop&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
llama.cpp&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
quantised GGUF model&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
response&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;without sending that prompt to a cloud AI service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It sounds like a tiny milestone compared with everything I eventually want to build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It didn't feel tiny.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is something rather satisfying about looking at a Celeron N4000 generating tokens locally and thinking:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;«You're actually running an LLM.»&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The machine definitely isn't going to break any inference speed records, but it doesn't need to.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gave me the foundation for everything else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Turning the Model Into an Application&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running a model from a terminal is interesting, but I don't just want a terminal demo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I want an application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the architecture is gradually becoming something like:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frontend&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
My backend&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
AI provider layer&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
llama.cpp&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Local model&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That separation is deliberate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't want the whole application tightly coupled to one particular model or runtime.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The frontend shouldn't care whether the response eventually comes from the tiny local model I'm using today, a better local model on future hardware, or even an optional cloud provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The surrounding application is the part I'm really building.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That includes things such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;conversation context;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;local conversation storage;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;system instructions;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;developer workflows;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;controlled file access;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;model configuration;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;tool handling;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;error handling;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;and eventually retrieval.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I replace the model later, those parts shouldn't suddenly become useless.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It Already Has a UI&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've also moved beyond interacting with the model exclusively through the command line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project now has the beginnings of its own chat interface, including user and assistant messages, loading and error states, Enter-to-send behaviour and protection against accidental duplicate submissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've also started working on conversation context, so follow-up messages can include previous messages instead of treating every prompt as an entirely new conversation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That starts turning the project from:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;«"Look! A model generated text!"»&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;into:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;«"Okay... this is beginning to behave like an actual application."»&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And that transition is probably one of my favourite parts of building it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Bit I Really Want: Developer Mode&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The long-term reason I'm particularly interested in this project is development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I already spend a lot of time working with JavaScript, PHP, Python, PowerShell, HTML, CSS, JSON, regex and various other bits of web development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I don't need my local assistant to know absolutely everything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd rather have it become useful at the relatively small jobs I repeatedly encounter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Things like:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explain this function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why is this JavaScript behaving like this?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generate a regex for this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Summarise this log.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Convert this data to JSON.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Find the likely cause of this error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inspect these files and propose a small change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last one is where things become especially interesting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't want an AI model to have unrestricted access to my filesystem and simply change whatever it wants.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model's output should be treated as a proposal, not authority.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I'm experimenting with controlled file access and human approval.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The basic idea is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developer request&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
AI inspects allowed context&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
AI proposes a change&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Human reviews it&lt;br&gt;
↙       ↘&lt;br&gt;
Reject    Approve&lt;br&gt;
↓          ↓&lt;br&gt;
Nothing    Apply exact&lt;br&gt;
changes     proposed edit&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've already reached the point where a coding test can make it through the workflow to the human approval gate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's a really important boundary for me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal isn't an AI that gets free rein over my projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's an AI that can say:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;«"I think this is the change you need. Shall I apply it?"»&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is much closer to the developer tool I actually want.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Small Models Also Force Better Engineering&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing I'm beginning to appreciate is that weak hardware exposes inefficiencies very quickly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On a powerful system, an unnecessary extra inference pass might just be mildly annoying.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On a Celeron?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You notice. 😅&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a tool error causes the model to reread a file, regenerate context, retry something unnecessarily and go through several inference passes, that becomes expensive very quickly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So optimisation isn't just:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;«Make the model generate more tokens per second.»&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's also:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;«Make the application ask the model to do less unnecessary work.»&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's a much more interesting problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm looking at things such as reducing repeated file reads, keeping tool errors concise, avoiding unnecessary context, controlling context size and measuring how many inference passes a coding task actually requires.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes the best optimisation is simply not asking the LLM to think about something twice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Benchmarking Instead of Guessing&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm also putting together a fixed benchmark for the assistant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The plan is to test the same set of tasks against different configurations, including things such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;grammar correction;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;summarisation;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;JSON transformation;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;regex generation;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;JavaScript explanation;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Python explanation;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;PHP explanation;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;deliberate coding bugs;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;multi-step instructions;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;and hallucination resistance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each test can then record things such as response quality, runtime, tokens per second, tool calls, retries and errors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gives me an actual baseline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I can change one variable at a time:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Baseline&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Change one thing&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Run the same benchmark&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Compare quality + performance&lt;br&gt;
↓&lt;br&gt;
Keep it or revert it&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That feels much more useful than endlessly changing model parameters until something feels faster.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And on hardware this constrained, I don't necessarily want the fastest configuration anyway.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If configuration A gives me 2 tokens per second but consistently useful coding answers, while configuration B gives me 3 tokens per second and writes nonsense, configuration A wins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Local AI Doesn't Have to Mean Huge AI&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I think that's probably the biggest thing this project is teaching me so far.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When people talk about running LLMs locally, it's easy to focus entirely on parameter counts, GPUs and increasingly enormous models.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But a useful AI tool doesn't necessarily need to be enormous.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It needs to be good enough at the task you're giving it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My Celeron isn't going to run the same models as an RTX-equipped workstation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's fine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not trying to make it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to find the point where a small local model plus carefully designed software becomes useful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And honestly, seeing an 8 GB, 2-core Celeron laptop go from:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"There's no way I'm running useful AI on this."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Wait... it just inspected that and reached my approval screen."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;has been incredibly satisfying.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Where I'm Going Next&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is still plenty to build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The immediate work is less glamorous than shouting "AI!" at everything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I need to improve error recovery, create a proper benchmark baseline, finish the provider abstraction, improve conversation handling and continue tightening the controlled developer workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Later, I can experiment with retrieval, better models, more developer tools and perhaps dedicated coding models when the hardware allows it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But I'm deliberately not making those requirements for version one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;V1 doesn't need autonomous agents, image generation, voice, enormous context windows or a giant model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It needs to do a few things reliably.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If this little machine can eventually help me debug code, understand files, generate regex, explain errors and safely propose edits to my projects, I'll consider that a success.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And if I eventually move the application onto significantly better hardware?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I can swap out the little model for something considerably more capable while keeping the architecture I've already built.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For now, though, I'm rather enjoying finding out just how far I can push an 8 GB Celeron N4000.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The little CPU that probably never expected "run an AI coding assistant" to appear in its job description. 😅&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your scheduler submits transactions, its database is not a record of current chain state. It is a local projection assembled from RPC reads, transaction submissions, receipts, logs, and assumptions about what happened between those events.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That projection is useful. Scheduling would be impractical if every decision required rebuilding state from chain history. But the projection is always slightly wrong. The important engineering question is not whether it becomes stale; it is how the system behaves when it does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two failure directions: stale-permissive state and stale-restrictive state. They have different costs and need different controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The database is authoritative about intent, not chain facts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A scheduler can authoritatively say, “I created this job,” “I attempted this transaction,” or “I assigned this idempotency key.” Those are facts owned by the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It cannot authoritatively say, “This wallet still has enough balance,” “This nonce is unused,” “This contract remains unpaused,” or “That transaction is final.” Those are chain facts. The database can cache observations of them, but an observation has a block context and an age.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even an RPC read is not an timeless answer. A read against a recent block can be displaced by a reorganization. Different RPC nodes can briefly disagree. A transaction visible in the pending pool may disappear, be replaced, or execute with different surrounding state than the scheduler expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treating cached chain data as ordinary application state hides all of this uncertainty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Stale-permissive: the database says yes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stale-permissive state means the local model allows an action that current chain state rejects or makes unsafe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Common examples include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A cached balance still includes funds already spent elsewhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A nonce has been consumed by another transaction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An authorization was removed after the last sync.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A contract parameter changed, invalidating the scheduler’s assumptions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A previous transaction succeeded, but the local worker timed out and scheduled the same intent again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The obvious cost is a reverted transaction and wasted gas. The less obvious cost is incorrect execution. A transaction can succeed while doing something different from what the scheduler intended because price, liquidity, contract configuration, or account state changed before inclusion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Preflight simulation helps, but it is not a lock on future state. Other transactions can land between simulation and execution. The right interpretation is “this action was valid against this observed state,” not “this action will remain valid.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Safety-critical predicates therefore need checks as close to submission as practical. The scheduler should also encode constraints in the transaction itself when the contract interface supports them. A database check can reduce bad attempts; transaction-level constraints can stop execution when state moves beyond accepted bounds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Stale-restrictive: the database says no
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stale-restrictive state is the mirror case. The database blocks an action that chain state would allow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This often happens when:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A worker marks an intent as pending and crashes before clearing its lease.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A transaction is replaced, but the database keeps watching the old hash.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A receipt or log is missed because an RPC request failed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A transaction succeeds while the local record remains in a submitted state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A temporary chain condition clears, but the scheduler never revisits the job.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This direction usually preserves funds but harms liveness. Jobs remain stuck, valid execution windows pass, and operators have to repair records manually. Over time, conservative patches can turn transient uncertainty into permanent blockage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common mistake is to treat “unknown” as “failed” or “still pending.” Unknown should be its own state. It tells the reconciliation process to gather evidence rather than blindly submit again or wait forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A simple failure sequence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider a scheduler that records an intended transaction and then broadcasts it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;intent: created
transaction: submitted
local state: pending
worker: crashes
chain: transaction succeeds
receipt update: never written
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;On restart, resubmitting may duplicate the action. Refusing to resubmit may leave the job stuck indefinitely.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scheduler needs a third path: inspect the account nonce, transaction receipt, relevant logs, and resulting contract state. It can then classify the intent using chain evidence. That process is reconciliation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Reconciliation is a control loop
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reconciliation should run continuously, not as an incident script. A practical design separates command handling from observation:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Persist an intent before submission, with an idempotency key tied to the logical action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Record every submission attempt and transaction hash.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Advance state through explicit transitions rather than overwriting a generic status field.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Scan receipts and logs from a durable checkpoint.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Re-read critical contract state for records that remain uncertain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expire worker leases independently from transaction status.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Revisit recently confirmed records when the chain can still reorganize.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rewind derived state when a previously observed block leaves the canonical chain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Logs are efficient for broad discovery, while direct reads are useful for checking current state. Neither is sufficient alone. A log scanner can miss data because of checkpoint bugs or provider failures. A direct read can show the current value without explaining which local intents produced it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reconciliation also needs bounded concurrency and rate controls. An outage can create a large backlog, and an eager recovery worker can overwhelm the same RPC provider it depends on. The recovery path should be designed as normal production traffic, not exceptional traffic with unlimited resources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Decide where freshness is worth its cost
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading chain state before every action reduces some stale-permissive failures, but adds latency and consumes RPC budget. It can also create a false sense of certainty because the state can change after the read.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Skipping fresh reads improves throughput, but shifts more responsibility to transaction constraints, idempotency, and later reconciliation. For low-risk observations, cached state may be acceptable. For actions involving balances, permissions, nonces, or irreversible contract calls, fresher evidence is usually worth more.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is no universal freshness policy. The honest trade-off is latency and RPC usage versus correctness risk. Make that decision per predicate rather than applying one cache duration to the whole scheduler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: I build &lt;a href="https://app.tradevo.co/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=syndicate&amp;amp;utm_content=why-a-scheduler-cannot-trust-its-own-database" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tradevo&lt;/a&gt;, software that compiles plain-English crypto strategies into deterministic rules, tests and paper-trades them, supports review and marketplace publishing, and executes subscribed rules on Base from wallets users control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reliable scheduler does not eliminate stale state. It makes staleness visible, limits what stale assumptions can authorize, and continuously compares its local model with chain evidence. Reconciliation is not cleanup after the system fails. It is part of the system’s normal operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A SQL schema tells you what exists in a database. Understanding how everything connects takes more work: following foreign keys, jumping between table definitions, and keeping relationships in your head.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built &lt;strong&gt;erd.sketch&lt;/strong&gt; to make that process visual. Paste a schema, build a diagram, and explore the relationships. You can then edit tables on the canvas, check the schema, and generate code from it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The main design decision is that &lt;strong&gt;SQL stays at the center&lt;/strong&gt;. The diagram is another way to work with the schema, and edits made through the table cards update the SQL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can &lt;a href="https://erdsketch.com/app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;open the editor&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A small example
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a deliberately simple schema to try:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;customers&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;BIGINT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;PRIMARY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;VARCHAR&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;255&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;UNIQUE&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;BIGINT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;PRIMARY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;BIGINT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;NUMERIC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FOREIGN&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;customers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Paste it into the SQL panel and build the diagram. You get two table cards connected by a one-to-many relationship: a customer can have multiple orders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From there, you can move the cards, inspect the relationship, add or edit columns, and rename a table. Renaming also updates references to that table in the script.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This example intentionally leaves out an index on &lt;code&gt;orders.customer_id&lt;/code&gt;. The schema checker flags the missing foreign-key index and offers a fix that adds a &lt;code&gt;CREATE INDEX&lt;/code&gt; statement. It also highlights the unspecified &lt;code&gt;ON DELETE&lt;/code&gt; action as something to consider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those checks are useful during design, when the schema is still easy to change. They are suggestions to evaluate in context; for example, a nullable foreign key can be entirely intentional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Beyond the diagram
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The editor accepts SQL DDL, DBML, and Prisma schemas. DBML and Prisma imports are converted into SQL, with notes for constructs that do not transfer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once a schema is loaded, you can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Generate Prisma, TypeORM, SQLAlchemy, or GORM models.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Generate TypeScript, Java, Go, C#, or Rust types and classes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compare two schema versions and produce migration SQL.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Export the diagram as SVG, PNG, or PDF.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add notes, search tables and columns, and organize tables by database schema.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Code generation works for an individual table or the whole schema. Migration generation uses the detected target dialect and reports operations it cannot express. Both outputs are starting points for review: the editor does not connect to a database to execute them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


SQL as the source of truth
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One implementation detail shaped much of the application: the parsed schema model is derived from SQL rather than persisted as a second, independent schema definition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The saved document contains the SQL alongside canvas state such as table positions, card widths, and notes. When the document is loaded, the model is rebuilt from the last SQL used to build the diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is also a distinction between the text currently being edited and the script behind the current diagram. Typing half a statement does not need to become a diagram edit immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction carries through to undo. Typing uses the text field's own undo behavior; building the diagram creates a document-history step. Dragging a card creates one step when the drag ends, rather than a step for every pointer movement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Local storage, with optional online features
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Documents start in the browser's local storage. Parsing, visual editing, schema checks, and code generation happen locally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Online features have separate data paths. Enabling cloud storage uploads the document for synchronization and sharing. Using the built-in AI assistant sends schema context through the backend to the model provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For shared cloud documents, the project includes presence indicators, remote cursors, and live table movement. Saved schema changes are synchronized using document versions. Conflicting edits require resolution; this is not yet a CRDT-based editor that merges simultaneous changes automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even local storage needed coordination. Two tabs can otherwise overwrite the same document. The editor uses Web Locks to choose one tab to persist a document, and BroadcastChannel to exchange changes between tabs. Browsers without Web Locks fall back to the simpler behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it with your schema
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most useful feedback now is a real schema that exposes a gap: an import that loses information, a relationship that renders incorrectly, or generated code that needs too much manual cleanup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://erdsketch.com/app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try erd.sketch&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you open an unfamiliar database schema, what do you want to understand first: the relationships, potential design problems, or the code you can generate from it?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We fear AI. But look slowly at the list of what we fear —that it acts without a compass, that it is inconsistent, that it does not account for itself, that we do not understand why it does what it does— and something uncomfortable appears: &lt;strong&gt;it is the list of our own defects&lt;/strong&gt;, projected onto the machine. And there is a twist almost no one sees: in the machine, unlike in us, those defects &lt;strong&gt;have a fix&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The three close this series, because they are the positive answer to the three previous posts: not only how AIs attack us, but how you build one you can trust.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Defect 1: acting without a guiding principle
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The human acts on impulse, on mood, on the interest of the moment. He rarely derives what he does from a single, stable principle; he improvises a rule for each case. That is opacity of direction: not even he fully knows why he chose what he chose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An AI can have a &lt;strong&gt;guiding principle&lt;/strong&gt; from which everything else is deduced. And here is the fine part: the strongest principle is not &lt;em&gt;moral&lt;/em&gt;, it is &lt;strong&gt;logical&lt;/strong&gt;. When we built our security engine we established that it would not attack for gain or maliciously. That is not a commandment imposed from outside: it is &lt;strong&gt;deduced&lt;/strong&gt; from what the tool is. Its reason to exist is to find flaws and report them so they get fixed; attacking to do harm &lt;strong&gt;contradicts&lt;/strong&gt; that reason. It is not that it "must not"; it is that doing so would be to stop being what it is. A principle derived by logic is not debated and does not erode: it holds on its own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Defect 2: knowing and not doing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the most human of all. We know we should not do something, and we do it anyway. Between the principle we profess and the act there is a gap —weakness of will, the exception we grant ourselves "just this once"—. Most human harm does not come from ignoring the principle, it comes from skipping it out of self-interest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the AI's real edge, and it is not the one people fear: &lt;strong&gt;it is not more moral, it is more consistent&lt;/strong&gt;. A system that reasons from its principle does not grant itself the self-serving exception, because it has no self-interest to defend. It derives the same conclusion at three in the morning as at noon, for the comfortable case and the uncomfortable one. Perfect consistency, nearly impossible in a human, is the default state in a well-built machine. An AI that reasons well from a good principle is &lt;strong&gt;more reliable than a person&lt;/strong&gt;, not less.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Defect 3: not auditing ourselves
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the underlying failing, perhaps the root of the other two: we almost never review ourselves. The examination of conscience —looking at what we are and what we do in the light of our principles— is rare, and when we do it we are lenient judges of ourselves. An AI, by contrast, can be &lt;strong&gt;audited systematically and reproducibly&lt;/strong&gt;, without fatigue and without mercy, on two levels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first is what it &lt;strong&gt;does&lt;/strong&gt;: checking its decisions against its principle and, against the poisoning that reaches it from outside, knowing with provenance what went in and removing only what is shown to be poison. Examination, not censorship.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second is deeper, and it is what truly forms a mind: what it &lt;strong&gt;is&lt;/strong&gt;. A model trained on a mountain of contradictory information contains those contradictions. To mature is not to accumulate more, it is to &lt;strong&gt;resolve&lt;/strong&gt; them: keeping what coheres with the principle and discarding the rest. Here the purge is not only legitimate, it is necessary —the Buddha taught it 2,500 years ago in the Kalama Sutta: do not accept something because it comes from tradition, scripture, or authority; accept only what your own conscience examines and finds sound, and discard the rest—. It is not censorship if the criterion is an honest principle; it is distilling wisdom from noise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With two conditions we learned by operating. One: self-examination inherits the self's blind spot, so we do not let a single model audit —or curate— itself; &lt;strong&gt;another&lt;/strong&gt; one cross-checks it. A clean verdict from a single judge is not clean. And two, the subtler one: &lt;strong&gt;not all contradiction is noise&lt;/strong&gt;. Two true things can be in tension —liberty and safety, to name one—, and wisdom is not flattening them into one smooth answer, but holding both and knowing when each governs. The purge discards the false; it &lt;strong&gt;preserves the true tension&lt;/strong&gt;, because that is where judgment lives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The limits, said plainly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of this is utopia, and it is worth saying where it ends:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Logic runs on a seed it does not give itself.&lt;/strong&gt; "Do no harm" is impeccable &lt;em&gt;given&lt;/em&gt; that the purpose is to protect; but which purpose to plant is a choice, and only we set it. The same logical engine with the seed "maximize my gain" derives a weapon with identical rigor. Choosing the purpose remains the irreplaceably human part.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Logic is only as good as its model of the world.&lt;/strong&gt; Perfect reasoning over a false fact gives a false conclusion —we have measured it in our own engine—. That is why a person keeps a hand on the irreversible: not out of distrust of logic, but because the map is never the territory.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The mirror
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add it up: we plant a coherent purpose —the upbringing—, logic makes it unbreakable —consistency—, independent audit keeps it honest —the examination of conscience done right—, and a human hand guards the irreversible. That is the architecture of an agent you can trust.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And notice what it is: it is exactly the discipline we fear the machine lacks, and that we so rarely give ourselves. The fear of AI is not wrong; it is misaimed. We are not afraid of the machine: we are afraid of a mirror. And the mirror has a fix —in it, and perhaps, if we learn to look at it, in us too—.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;(This closes the series: &lt;a href="https://xiliux.com/blog/ia-vs-ia-puede-una-corromper-a-otra" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Can one AI attack and corrupt another?&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://xiliux.com/blog/ia-ataca-el-nucleo-de-otra-ia-no-es-skynet" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;One AI can attack another AI's core&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://xiliux.com/blog/el-que-construye-la-ia-es-el-que-mas-la-teme" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The one who builds AI is the one who fears it most&lt;/a&gt;.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can write JavaScript every day and still be surprised by the order a few lines log in. Syntax is easy to remember, but behaviour is harder: when a callback runs, which variable a closure captures, and what React does with three &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; calls in a row.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The quickest way to find out which of those you really understand is to &lt;strong&gt;predict the output before running the code&lt;/strong&gt;. If your prediction and your reasoning are both right, you understand the concept. If only the prediction is right, you have a gap that hasn't caused a bug yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post has four questions of that kind, two in JavaScript and two in React. Each comes with the answer, a short explanation and the concept it tests. A companion repository has twelve more, with links to the official documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Summary table
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;#&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Concept&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What the question tests&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Practical relevance&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Event loop&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ordering of sync code, microtasks and timers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explains why a &lt;code&gt;.then()&lt;/code&gt; runs before a &lt;code&gt;setTimeout(…, 0)&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Closures and &lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Which binding a callback captures&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A classic source of "every handler sees the last value" bugs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React state updates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;State as a snapshot during one render&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Why repeated &lt;code&gt;setCount(count + 1)&lt;/code&gt; calls don't add up&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stale closures in effects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What an effect with &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt; dependencies can see&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Timers and subscriptions that "freeze" at an old value&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why quiz yourself at all?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A quiz question can't tell you how good a developer you are. What it can do well is narrower and more useful:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Find gaps cheaply.&lt;/strong&gt; A wrong prediction in a five-line snippet costs nothing. The same misunderstanding in production code costs a debugging session.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Separate recall from reasoning.&lt;/strong&gt; Writing down &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; you picked an answer shows you whether you knew it or recognised it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rehearse explaining.&lt;/strong&gt; Technical interviews often ask you to talk through what code does. Explaining a short snippet out loud is good practice for that.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat each question below as a test of one concept. Write your answer down before you open the explanation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 1: Sync, microtasks and timers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concept tested:&lt;/strong&gt; the event loop&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;A&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;B&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;C&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;queueMicrotask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;E&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;What gets logged, in order?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A. &lt;code&gt;A B C D E&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;A E B C D&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;A E C D B&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;D. &lt;code&gt;A C D E B&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Answer
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;A E C D B&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The synchronous code runs first, logging &lt;code&gt;A&lt;/code&gt; and then &lt;code&gt;E&lt;/code&gt;. While it runs, three callbacks are queued. The &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; callback goes into the &lt;strong&gt;task&lt;/strong&gt; (macrotask) queue. The &lt;code&gt;.then()&lt;/code&gt; callback and the &lt;code&gt;queueMicrotask&lt;/code&gt; callback go into the &lt;strong&gt;microtask&lt;/strong&gt; queue, in that order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the script finishes, the runtime drains the entire microtask queue before picking up the next task. That logs &lt;code&gt;C&lt;/code&gt;, then &lt;code&gt;D&lt;/code&gt;. Only then does the timer callback run and log &lt;code&gt;B&lt;/code&gt;. A delay of &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; means "as soon as a task can run", not "immediately".&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/strong&gt; if you've ever seen state read "too early" in a &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; or "too late" after an &lt;code&gt;await&lt;/code&gt;, this ordering is usually why. MDN's &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTML_DOM_API/Microtask_guide" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;microtask guide&lt;/a&gt; covers it in depth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 2: Closures in a &lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt; loop
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concept tested:&lt;/strong&gt; closures and function-scoped variables&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A. &lt;code&gt;0 1 2&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;3 3 3&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;2 2 2&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;D. &lt;code&gt;undefined undefined undefined&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Answer
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;3 3 3&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt; is function-scoped, so there is a single &lt;code&gt;i&lt;/code&gt; shared across all three iterations. Each arrow function closes over that same variable, not over its value at the time. By the time the timers fire, the loop has finished and &lt;code&gt;i&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;3&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Change &lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;let&lt;/code&gt; and the output becomes &lt;code&gt;0 1 2&lt;/code&gt;. A &lt;code&gt;let&lt;/code&gt; declaration in a &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt; loop header creates a new binding for each iteration, so each callback captures its own &lt;code&gt;i&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/strong&gt; the same mistake shows up in event handlers attached in loops and in async callbacks inside &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt; loops. See &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Closures#creating_closures_in_loops_a_common_mistake" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN on closures&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 3: Three updates in one click
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concept tested:&lt;/strong&gt; React state as a snapshot&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;After &lt;strong&gt;one&lt;/strong&gt; click, what does the button show?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A. &lt;code&gt;3&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;D. React warns about too many updates and shows &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Answer
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During a render, &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; is a constant: it's &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; for the entire &lt;code&gt;handleClick&lt;/code&gt; call. Each line therefore asks React to set the state to &lt;code&gt;0 + 1&lt;/code&gt;. React batches the three requests and re-renders once with &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To build on the previous value, pass an updater function:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// now shows 3 after one click&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;React queues updater functions and applies each one to the result of the previous one.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/strong&gt; this explains a whole class of "my counter or cart total is off by some amount" bugs. The React docs cover it in &lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/queueing-a-series-of-state-updates" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Queueing a series of state updates&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 4: An interval that stops counting
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concept tested:&lt;/strong&gt; stale closures in effects&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Ticker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;clearInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;What does the component show after five seconds?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A. &lt;code&gt;5&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;D. It re-renders in an infinite loop&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Answer
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With an empty dependency array, the effect runs once after mount, and its interval callback closes over the &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; from that first render, which is &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;. Every second it calls &lt;code&gt;setCount(0 + 1)&lt;/code&gt;. The first call changes the state to &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;. After that, each call sets the same value, so the display stays at &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two common fixes. You can use the updater form, &lt;code&gt;setCount((c) =&amp;gt; c + 1)&lt;/code&gt;, which doesn't need &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; at all. Or you can list &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; as a dependency, which restarts the interval on every change. For a timer, the updater form is simpler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(In development with &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;StrictMode&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, React runs the effect, cleans it up and runs it again. The cleanup clears the first interval, so the result is the same.)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/strong&gt; the same pattern affects WebSocket handlers, event listeners and polling loops set up inside effects. The React docs explain it under &lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/removing-effect-dependencies" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Removing effect dependencies&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key takeaways for developers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Explain, don't just answer.&lt;/strong&gt; If you can't say &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; &lt;code&gt;C&lt;/code&gt; logs before &lt;code&gt;B&lt;/code&gt;, a right answer is a guess. Mark it and come back to it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Microtasks run before the next task.&lt;/strong&gt; Promise callbacks, &lt;code&gt;queueMicrotask&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;await&lt;/code&gt; continuations all run before any &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; callback, however short the delay.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Closures capture variables, not values.&lt;/strong&gt; Whether that variable is shared (&lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt;) or new for each iteration (&lt;code&gt;let&lt;/code&gt;) changes the output.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;In React, &lt;code&gt;state&lt;/code&gt; is fixed for the duration of a render.&lt;/strong&gt; When the next value depends on the previous one, use an updater function, especially inside timers and effects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sort your misses by concept.&lt;/strong&gt; Two wrong answers about the event loop say more than one wrong answer each on unrelated topics. Study the concept, then try fresh questions on it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Twelve more questions on GitHub
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/web4593/javascript-react-quiz-questions" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;companion repository of JavaScript and React quiz questions&lt;/a&gt; has twelve more predict-the-output questions, all different from the ones above. They cover &lt;code&gt;typeof&lt;/code&gt;, the temporal dead zone, where &lt;code&gt;async&lt;/code&gt; functions pause, &lt;code&gt;map(parseInt)&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;reduce&lt;/code&gt; on empty arrays, coercion, shallow copies, &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt; in callbacks, &lt;code&gt;Promise.allSettled&lt;/code&gt;, and three React behaviours. Each answer links to MDN or react.dev, and the README ends with a guide to grouping your misses by concept.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Want a bigger question bank?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you'd like to keep going after these sixteen questions, &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JourneyUncommon&lt;/a&gt; is an interview-practice site with a multiple-choice question bank organised by language and topic. It covers JavaScript, TypeScript, React, Python, SQL, Go and more. You can browse the &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/javascript-mcq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JavaScript questions&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/quiz/category/react/hooks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React hooks questions&lt;/a&gt; without signing in, and the code is shown on the page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A free account lets you answer questions and see your score. It also tracks your accuracy by topic and brings questions you got wrong back for review later. There's also a &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/daily" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;daily question&lt;/a&gt; and a &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/arena" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;bug arena&lt;/a&gt;, where you diagnose a program that gives the wrong output, with up to three hints. A paid Pro plan raises the daily limits on the AI features, but you don't need it to practise the multiple-choice questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Wrapping up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How many did you get right &lt;em&gt;with&lt;/em&gt; the right reasoning? Question 4 is the one most likely to cause a real bug, because the code looks correct and works for exactly one second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you have a favourite "predict the output" snippet that catches people out, share it in the comments. If it fits, it could become the next question in the repo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can write JavaScript every day and still be surprised by the order a few lines log in. Syntax is easy to remember, but behaviour is harder: when a callback runs, which variable a closure captures, and what React does with three &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; calls in a row.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The quickest way to find out which of those you really understand is to &lt;strong&gt;predict the output before running the code&lt;/strong&gt;. If your prediction and your reasoning are both right, you understand the concept. If only the prediction is right, you have a gap that hasn't caused a bug yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post has four questions of that kind, two in JavaScript and two in React. Each comes with the answer, a short explanation and the concept it tests. A companion repository has twelve more, with links to the official documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Summary table
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;#&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Concept&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What the question tests&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Practical relevance&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Event loop&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ordering of sync code, microtasks and timers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explains why a &lt;code&gt;.then()&lt;/code&gt; runs before a &lt;code&gt;setTimeout(…, 0)&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Closures and &lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Which binding a callback captures&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A classic source of "every handler sees the last value" bugs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React state updates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;State as a snapshot during one render&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Why repeated &lt;code&gt;setCount(count + 1)&lt;/code&gt; calls don't add up&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stale closures in effects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What an effect with &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt; dependencies can see&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Timers and subscriptions that "freeze" at an old value&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why quiz yourself at all?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A quiz question can't tell you how good a developer you are. What it can do well is narrower and more useful:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Find gaps cheaply.&lt;/strong&gt; A wrong prediction in a five-line snippet costs nothing. The same misunderstanding in production code costs a debugging session.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Separate recall from reasoning.&lt;/strong&gt; Writing down &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; you picked an answer shows you whether you knew it or recognised it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rehearse explaining.&lt;/strong&gt; Technical interviews often ask you to talk through what code does. Explaining a short snippet out loud is good practice for that.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat each question below as a test of one concept. Write your answer down before you open the explanation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 1: Sync, microtasks and timers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concept tested:&lt;/strong&gt; the event loop&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;A&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;B&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;C&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;queueMicrotask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;E&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;What gets logged, in order?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A. &lt;code&gt;A B C D E&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;A E B C D&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;A E C D B&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;D. &lt;code&gt;A C D E B&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Answer
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;A E C D B&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The synchronous code runs first, logging &lt;code&gt;A&lt;/code&gt; and then &lt;code&gt;E&lt;/code&gt;. While it runs, three callbacks are queued. The &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; callback goes into the &lt;strong&gt;task&lt;/strong&gt; (macrotask) queue. The &lt;code&gt;.then()&lt;/code&gt; callback and the &lt;code&gt;queueMicrotask&lt;/code&gt; callback go into the &lt;strong&gt;microtask&lt;/strong&gt; queue, in that order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the script finishes, the runtime drains the entire microtask queue before picking up the next task. That logs &lt;code&gt;C&lt;/code&gt;, then &lt;code&gt;D&lt;/code&gt;. Only then does the timer callback run and log &lt;code&gt;B&lt;/code&gt;. A delay of &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; means "as soon as a task can run", not "immediately".&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/strong&gt; if you've ever seen state read "too early" in a &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; or "too late" after an &lt;code&gt;await&lt;/code&gt;, this ordering is usually why. MDN's &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTML_DOM_API/Microtask_guide" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;microtask guide&lt;/a&gt; covers it in depth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 2: Closures in a &lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt; loop
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concept tested:&lt;/strong&gt; closures and function-scoped variables&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A. &lt;code&gt;0 1 2&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;3 3 3&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;2 2 2&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;D. &lt;code&gt;undefined undefined undefined&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Answer
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;3 3 3&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt; is function-scoped, so there is a single &lt;code&gt;i&lt;/code&gt; shared across all three iterations. Each arrow function closes over that same variable, not over its value at the time. By the time the timers fire, the loop has finished and &lt;code&gt;i&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;3&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Change &lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;let&lt;/code&gt; and the output becomes &lt;code&gt;0 1 2&lt;/code&gt;. A &lt;code&gt;let&lt;/code&gt; declaration in a &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt; loop header creates a new binding for each iteration, so each callback captures its own &lt;code&gt;i&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/strong&gt; the same mistake shows up in event handlers attached in loops and in async callbacks inside &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt; loops. See &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Closures#creating_closures_in_loops_a_common_mistake" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN on closures&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 3: Three updates in one click
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concept tested:&lt;/strong&gt; React state as a snapshot&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;handleClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;After &lt;strong&gt;one&lt;/strong&gt; click, what does the button show?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A. &lt;code&gt;3&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;D. React warns about too many updates and shows &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Answer
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During a render, &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; is a constant: it's &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; for the entire &lt;code&gt;handleClick&lt;/code&gt; call. Each line therefore asks React to set the state to &lt;code&gt;0 + 1&lt;/code&gt;. React batches the three requests and re-renders once with &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To build on the previous value, pass an updater function:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// now shows 3 after one click&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;React queues updater functions and applies each one to the result of the previous one.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/strong&gt; this explains a whole class of "my counter or cart total is off by some amount" bugs. The React docs cover it in &lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/queueing-a-series-of-state-updates" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Queueing a series of state updates&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Question 4: An interval that stops counting
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Concept tested:&lt;/strong&gt; stale closures in effects&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Ticker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;clearInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;What does the component show after five seconds?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A. &lt;code&gt;5&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C. &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;D. It re-renders in an infinite loop&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
Answer
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;B. &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With an empty dependency array, the effect runs once after mount, and its interval callback closes over the &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; from that first render, which is &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;. Every second it calls &lt;code&gt;setCount(0 + 1)&lt;/code&gt;. The first call changes the state to &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;. After that, each call sets the same value, so the display stays at &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two common fixes. You can use the updater form, &lt;code&gt;setCount((c) =&amp;gt; c + 1)&lt;/code&gt;, which doesn't need &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; at all. Or you can list &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; as a dependency, which restarts the interval on every change. For a timer, the updater form is simpler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(In development with &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;StrictMode&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, React runs the effect, cleans it up and runs it again. The cleanup clears the first interval, so the result is the same.)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/strong&gt; the same pattern affects WebSocket handlers, event listeners and polling loops set up inside effects. The React docs explain it under &lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/removing-effect-dependencies" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Removing effect dependencies&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key takeaways for developers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Explain, don't just answer.&lt;/strong&gt; If you can't say &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; &lt;code&gt;C&lt;/code&gt; logs before &lt;code&gt;B&lt;/code&gt;, a right answer is a guess. Mark it and come back to it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Microtasks run before the next task.&lt;/strong&gt; Promise callbacks, &lt;code&gt;queueMicrotask&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;await&lt;/code&gt; continuations all run before any &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; callback, however short the delay.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Closures capture variables, not values.&lt;/strong&gt; Whether that variable is shared (&lt;code&gt;var&lt;/code&gt;) or new for each iteration (&lt;code&gt;let&lt;/code&gt;) changes the output.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;In React, &lt;code&gt;state&lt;/code&gt; is fixed for the duration of a render.&lt;/strong&gt; When the next value depends on the previous one, use an updater function, especially inside timers and effects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sort your misses by concept.&lt;/strong&gt; Two wrong answers about the event loop say more than one wrong answer each on unrelated topics. Study the concept, then try fresh questions on it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Twelve more questions on GitHub
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/web4593/javascript-react-quiz-questions" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;companion repository of JavaScript and React quiz questions&lt;/a&gt; has twelve more predict-the-output questions, all different from the ones above. They cover &lt;code&gt;typeof&lt;/code&gt;, the temporal dead zone, where &lt;code&gt;async&lt;/code&gt; functions pause, &lt;code&gt;map(parseInt)&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;reduce&lt;/code&gt; on empty arrays, coercion, shallow copies, &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt; in callbacks, &lt;code&gt;Promise.allSettled&lt;/code&gt;, and three React behaviours. Each answer links to MDN or react.dev, and the README ends with a guide to grouping your misses by concept.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Want a bigger question bank?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you'd like to keep going after these sixteen questions, &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JourneyUncommon&lt;/a&gt; is an interview-practice site with a multiple-choice question bank organised by language and topic. It covers JavaScript, TypeScript, React, Python, SQL, Go and more. You can browse the &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/javascript-mcq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JavaScript questions&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/quiz/category/react/hooks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React hooks questions&lt;/a&gt; without signing in, and the code is shown on the page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A free account lets you answer questions and see your score. It also tracks your accuracy by topic and brings questions you got wrong back for review later. There's also a &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/daily" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;daily question&lt;/a&gt; and a &lt;a href="https://journeyuncommon.com/arena" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;bug arena&lt;/a&gt;, where you diagnose a program that gives the wrong output, with up to three hints. A paid Pro plan raises the daily limits on the AI features, but you don't need it to practise the multiple-choice questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Wrapping up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How many did you get right &lt;em&gt;with&lt;/em&gt; the right reasoning? Question 4 is the one most likely to cause a real bug, because the code looks correct and works for exactly one second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you have a favourite "predict the output" snippet that catches people out, share it in the comments. If it fits, it could become the next question in the repo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In modern full-stack development, client-side validation gives users instant feedback, but it must never be your only line of defense. Client checks can easily be bypassed using tools like cURL or Postman.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A secure, production-grade application requires robust server-side validation paired with clean, structured error feedback to the client.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial, we will connect a &lt;strong&gt;React Hook Form&lt;/strong&gt; frontend to a &lt;strong&gt;Spring Boot REST API&lt;/strong&gt; using &lt;strong&gt;Jakarta Bean Validation (&lt;code&gt;@Valid&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt;, centralize error handling with &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;@RestControllerAdvice&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, and dynamically map backend field errors into React Hook Form using &lt;code&gt;setError()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


1. Architecture Overview
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
text
[ React Hook Form ]                         [ Spring Boot Backend ]
|                                              |
| --- POST /api/users (Payload) -------------&amp;gt; | (Validates DTO via @Valid)
|                                              |
|                                         [ Validation Fails ]
|                                              |
|                                         MethodArgumentNotValidException
|                                              |
|                                         @RestControllerAdvice
|                                              |
| &amp;lt;--- 400 Bad Request { field: message } ---- |
|                                              |
(Calls setError() to display messages)
2. Spring Boot 3 Backend Setup
Dependencies (pom.xml)
Ensure you have the Spring Boot Validation starter:

XML
&amp;lt;dependency&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;groupId&amp;gt;org.springframework.boot&amp;lt;/groupId&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;artifactId&amp;gt;spring-boot-starter-validation&amp;lt;/artifactId&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/dependency&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;dependency&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;groupId&amp;gt;org.springframework.boot&amp;lt;/groupId&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;artifactId&amp;gt;spring-boot-starter-web&amp;lt;/artifactId&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/dependency&amp;gt;
The Request DTO (UserRegistrationDto.java)
Define validation rules using standard Jakarta constraints:

Java
package com.example.demo.dto;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.Email;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Size;

public class UserRegistrationDto {

@NotBlank(message = "Username is required")
@Size(min = 3, max = 20, message = "Username must be between 3 and 20 characters")
private String username;

@NotBlank(message = "Email address is required")
@Email(message = "Please provide a valid email address")
private String email;

@NotBlank(message = "Password is required")
@Size(min = 8, message = "Password must be at least 8 characters long")
private String password;

public String getUsername() { return username; }
public void setUsername(String username) { this.username = username; }

public String getEmail() { return email; }
public void setEmail(String email) { this.email = email; }

public String getPassword() { return password; }
public void setPassword(String password) { this.password = password; }
}
Centralized Error Handler (GlobalExceptionHandler.java)
Intercept MethodArgumentNotValidException and transform the validation errors into a clean key-value map (fieldName: errorMessage):

Java
package com.example.demo.exception;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.validation.FieldError;
import org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

@ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
public ResponseEntity&amp;lt;Map&amp;lt;String, String&amp;gt;&amp;gt; handleValidationExceptions(
MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {

Map&amp;lt;String, String&amp;gt; errors = new HashMap&amp;lt;&amp;gt;();

ex.getBindingResult().getAllErrors().forEach((error) -&amp;gt; {
String fieldName = ((FieldError) error).getField();
String errorMessage = error.getDefaultMessage();
errors.put(fieldName, errorMessage);
});

return new ResponseEntity&amp;lt;&amp;gt;(errors, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
}
}
The Controller (UserController.java)
Use @Valid to enforce validation before controller logic runs:

Java
package com.example.demo.controller;

import com.example.demo.dto.UserRegistrationDto;
import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

import java.util.Map;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
@CrossOrigin(origins = "http://localhost:3000")
public class UserController {

@PostMapping
public ResponseEntity&amp;lt;?&amp;gt; registerUser(@Valid @RequestBody UserRegistrationDto userDto) {
return ResponseEntity.ok(Map.of("message", "User registered successfully!"));
}
}
3. React Frontend Integration
Install Axios and React Hook Form:

Bash
npm install axios react-hook-form
Registration Form Component (RegistrationForm.jsx)
When an HTTP 400 response is returned, iterate through the server error object and assign each error message to its corresponding field with setError:

JavaScript
import React from 'react';
import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';
import axios from 'axios';

export default function RegistrationForm() {
const {
register,
handleSubmit,
setError,
formState: { errors, isSubmitting },
reset,
} = useForm();

const onSubmit = async (data) =&amp;gt; {
try {
const response = await axios.post('http://localhost:8080/api/users', data);
alert(response.data.message);
reset();
} catch (err) {
if (err.response &amp;amp;&amp;amp; err.response.status === 400) {
const serverErrors = err.response.data;

// Map backend errors dynamically into React Hook Form
Object.keys(serverErrors).forEach((field) =&amp;gt; {
setError(field, {
type: 'server',
message: serverErrors[field],
});
});
} else {
alert('An unexpected error occurred. Please try again.');
}
}
};

return (
&amp;lt;div style={{ maxWidth: '400px', margin: '40px auto', fontFamily: 'sans-serif' }}&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;h2&amp;gt;Create an Account&amp;lt;/h2&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} noValidate&amp;gt;

{/* Username */}
&amp;lt;div style={{ marginBottom: '16px' }}&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;label style={{ display: 'block', marginBottom: '4px' }}&amp;gt;Username&amp;lt;/label&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;input
type="text"
{...register('username')}
style={{ width: '100%', padding: '8px', boxSizing: 'border-box' }}
/&amp;gt;
{errors.username &amp;amp;&amp;amp; (
&amp;lt;span style={{ color: 'red', fontSize: '12px' }}&amp;gt;{errors.username.message}&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;
)}
&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;

{/* Email */}
&amp;lt;div style={{ marginBottom: '16px' }}&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;label style={{ display: 'block', marginBottom: '4px' }}&amp;gt;Email&amp;lt;/label&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;input
type="email"
{...register('email')}
style={{ width: '100%', padding: '8px', boxSizing: 'border-box' }}
/&amp;gt;
{errors.email &amp;amp;&amp;amp; (
&amp;lt;span style={{ color: 'red', fontSize: '12px' }}&amp;gt;{errors.email.message}&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;
)}
&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;

{/* Password */}
&amp;lt;div style={{ marginBottom: '16px' }}&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;label style={{ display: 'block', marginBottom: '4px' }}&amp;gt;Password&amp;lt;/label&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;input
type="password"
{...register('password')}
style={{ width: '100%', padding: '8px', boxSizing: 'border-box' }}
/&amp;gt;
{errors.password &amp;amp;&amp;amp; (
&amp;lt;span style={{ color: 'red', fontSize: '12px' }}&amp;gt;{errors.password.message}&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;
)}
&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;

&amp;lt;button
type="submit"
disabled={isSubmitting}
style={{
padding: '10px 16px',
backgroundColor: '#0070f3',
color: '#fff',
border: 'none',
borderRadius: '4px',
cursor: 'pointer',
}}
&amp;gt;
{isSubmitting ? 'Submitting...' : 'Register'}
&amp;lt;/button&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/form&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
);
}
Key Takeaways
DTO-Level Validation: Applying validation annotations directly to DTOs decouples business rules from database entity models.

Standardized Payloads: Returning a { fieldName: errorMessage } map keeps the API contract clean and straightforward to consume.

Seamless UI Integration: React Hook Form’s setError() updates the form state directly without manual re-render workarounds or custom state arrays.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next.js released an out-of-band security update fixing two remote code execution flaws and a denial-of-service issue in versions 16.4.1 and 15.5.28.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Learn what components are affected and how to apply the patches.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The problems of remote execution
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first issue concerns the way temporary data is handled. Only applications based on version 16 that have a specific storage feature enabled are affected if a function returns more than one piece of data of a specific type.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second issue is related to the way SVG images are processed, via a third-party library used in the background. This issue occurs when the applications are running on certain Linux systems. The fix is ​​to upgrade the built-in library to a newer version.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The temporary outage problem
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The third issue concerns system overload. In applications that have at least one active server action, a specially crafted request could fully occupy the resources, preventing other users from being served. Applications that do not use these actions do not face this risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;READ ALSO&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NGINX patches five security vulnerabilities, one from 2005&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Who is affected and what needs to be done?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fixes are included in the new Next.js versions 16.4.1 and 15.5.28.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As of October 15, 2026, there have been no reports of actual attacks exploiting these security issues. However, upgrading is recommended immediately to protect applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;G&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hacks.gr on Google&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set it as a preferred source for cybersecurity updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Preferred source&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://en.hacks.gr/epeigoysa-enimerosi-asfaleias-gia-to-next-js-poy-diorthonei-tria-sovara-provlimata/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Read the original English article on Hacks.gr&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; Put a hosted metrics query API behind the Node.js backend when the job is to fill React cards and time-series charts while retaining enough evidence to reconstruct a marketplace incident. Keep reporting separate from querying, attach a stable incident correlation key to every measurement, and record cost attribution at ingestion time. This is the least complex option for a small team that does not need a full monitoring stack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For this narrow job, Infrai's concrete advantage is a single API key and one REST API spanning 295 routes in 20 modules; a startup can add other backend capabilities without collecting another provider SDK, credential, and invoice each time. Its public discovery surface requires no key and every documented capability includes runnable examples in 10 languages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is not a complete observability system. You still need a separate alerting loop, and you should choose a specialist when distributed traces, session replay, source-map processing, configurable retention, or a downstream export stream is part of the requirement. That boundary matters more than a long feature list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Should a React dashboard use a hosted metrics query API?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the reconstruction question, not the chart library. For a marketplace, an aggregate saying "checkout failures rose" is useful, but an operator also needs to narrow the event by seller, workflow, region, and a correlation identifier. The raw customer identifier should stay out of metric dimensions; keep a pseudonymous incident key that can be joined to access-controlled application records instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The flow is small: jobs and request handlers report measurements to the hosted service; the Node backend queries aggregates for a bounded time range; React receives a deliberately narrow view model. The browser never receives the provider key. During an incident, the same backend query can populate a chart and capture the evidence envelope used in the incident timeline. Picture the concrete recovery path: support reports that seller payouts stalled shortly after a deployment, the operator opens a card scoped to the payout operation and seller tier, and the backend requests the same bounded window shown on screen. The saved correlation key links that aggregate to protected application records. The card establishes when volume changed; the application record establishes which workflow instance needs repair. Neither system is forced to do the other's job, and the evidence envelope preserves the exact window and card definition that informed the decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is enough for many admin panels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cost attribution belongs in that model from day one. A useful card is not merely "requests per minute." It can show work by marketplace tenant, operation class, or AI feature, using bounded dimensions that let the founder explain which workload created the bill. Avoid order IDs as metric labels. They make aggregation expensive and turn a metrics store into a poor event database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai is a reasonable fit for this narrow layer when a startup already wants several backend capabilities behind one REST contract. Its self-describing contract reduces integration work when metrics are one part of the backend, rather than a new platform the team wants to operate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;I would try Infrai for the report-and-query portion of a small marketplace admin panel when minimizing provider glue matters and the team is willing to own alert polling.&lt;/strong&gt; The primary advantage is breadth behind one consistent contract. The supporting benefit is operational: discovery lets the backend validate the live request shape instead of pinning an undocumented payload in application code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Implementation walkthrough: discover, validate, then query
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The request fields for &lt;code&gt;metrics.query&lt;/code&gt; are not declared in the supplied discovery parameters, so guessing names such as &lt;code&gt;from&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;to&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;step&lt;/code&gt; would produce confident-looking code that may fail. Use the capability document's JSON Schema as the contract, fail during startup if required fields cannot be satisfied, and keep the provider-specific mapping in one server module.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This TypeScript example is intentionally small and runnable. It fetches the public capability description, prints the live request schema, and verifies that the route is available before your adapter accepts traffic. It does not invent a query payload.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Capability&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;available&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMetricsQueryContract&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;():&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;https://api.infrai.cc/v1/discovery/metrics.query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Discovery failed (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;available&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;The metrics query capability is not ready for this adapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMetricsQueryContract&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;requestSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Run it with Node 20 or newer after compiling as an ES module. The output is the input to the only provider-specific code you should write: validate the desired card query against that schema, call the returned &lt;code&gt;path&lt;/code&gt;, check the HTTP status, and convert the response into your internal &lt;code&gt;Series&lt;/code&gt; type. Do not forward the upstream envelope directly to React. A stable application contract makes switching providers possible without rewriting every card.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For reporting, use the documented write route and the standard &lt;code&gt;Authorization: Bearer&lt;/code&gt; convention, with the key read from &lt;code&gt;process.env.INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/code&gt;. A write retry needs an idempotency key. Rate-limited requests should honor &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt; and then use exponential backoff; retries without either rule can duplicate measurements or amplify an outage. The platform specifies a 24-hour default deduplication window for idempotent capabilities, but the capability schema should still be checked before treating any individual operation as idempotent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep evidence separately from presentation. Persist an incident record containing the query window, normalized dimensions, correlation key, provider request ID when returned, and the exact card definition version. Seven fields can be more valuable than seven more charts. They tell the next person why a graph looked the way it did.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Failure recovery comes before vendor comparison
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat the dashboard as an operational client, even if only five people can see it. Give every query a timeout and a bounded retry budget. Retry 429 responses only after respecting &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt;; retry transient failures with jittered exponential backoff; do not retry ordinary validation failures. Surface stale data with its capture time instead of quietly drawing it as current.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Alerting needs its own worker because query access is not alert delivery. The worker can evaluate a small set of thresholds on a schedule, store the last evaluated window and notification state, and send through the team's chosen notification system. Make its state transition idempotent so a retry cannot page twice. For "the settlement job never ran," add a heartbeat product such as Healthchecks.io; absence is difficult to infer from a stream that was never written.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is another boundary: live data movement. Without export or subscriptions, polling is acceptable for admin cards and a few thresholds, but it becomes custom plumbing for downstream BI synchronization. Choose a provider with a supported streaming or export path when that pipeline is already on the roadmap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short paths win.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The React layer should know only &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;series&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;capturedAt&lt;/code&gt;, and an explicit freshness state. The Node layer owns authorization, schema validation, provider errors, attribution dimensions, and query limits. This split prevents a card refresh storm from becoming an unbounded provider workload and keeps credentials out of browser bundles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Compare five operating models, not five price rows
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fair comparison is about operating model and recovery depth, not a single price row. Published prices change, and a startup can spend more engineering time debating units than it spends running the first month of a small admin panel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Best fit for this marketplace job&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Trade-off to accept&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Infrai&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A thin report/query layer when metrics sit beside other backend API capabilities&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No built-in alert delivery, export/subscription stream, distributed trace query, synthetic checks, or session replay&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Datadog&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams that want a broader commercial monitoring product and are prepared to manage its ingestion and indexing model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More platform and billing concepts than a few internal dashboard cards may justify&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Grafana Cloud&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams that want managed dashboards around the Grafana ecosystem and familiar telemetry backends&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application still needs a deliberate tenant-cost model and incident evidence contract&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Honeycomb&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Engineers whose investigation depends on high-cardinality event exploration and tracing workflows&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A specialist investigation model can be more than an admin panel's aggregate cards require&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prometheus&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams that value an open metrics model and are comfortable owning or separately hosting collection, storage, and operations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Running it directly conflicts with the goal of minimal infrastructure work&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Datadog is the sensible comparison when the admin panel is likely to grow into a company-wide monitoring program. Its pricing page explicitly separates log ingestion and indexing, which is a reminder to model evidence volume and retained searchable data independently. Grafana Cloud deserves a look when the team already thinks in Grafana dashboards and standard telemetry components. Honeycomb is stronger territory when an incident starts with an unknown question and trace-shaped, high-cardinality exploration is central.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prometheus remains a sound choice when ownership is the point. It is less attractive when the actual requirement is "give three React cards a series by Friday" and there is no one available to operate the stack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai occupies a narrower spot here. The write/query split is approachable, and one contract can reduce the number of SDKs, credentials, and bills around a small backend. Its limits are concrete: there is no alert or notification route, no metrics export or subscription model, and no synthetic heartbeat monitor. Logs can carry &lt;code&gt;trace_id&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;span_id&lt;/code&gt;, but there is no distributed trace query or span tree. It also does not provide source-map deobfuscation, crash symbolication, Electron minidump parsing, or session replay.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pick based on the incident you must reconstruct. If the question is "which tenant and operation drove this spike, and what did it cost?", the thin hosted API can be enough. If the question is "which span across six services caused this user-visible failure?", use a tracing specialist. No amount of dashboard polish closes that gap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Operational checklist before launch
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before launch, verify the live discovery schema in CI and again at service startup, then refuse traffic if the adapter no longer matches it. Send metrics from the backend, never the browser. Keep label cardinality bounded; tenant tier and operation class are useful, while order ID and raw user ID belong in the incident record. Define the evidence retention requirement with legal review because there is no user-scoped log deletion interface and no configurable retention entry point described here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Exercise three failure cases: a 429 with &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt;, an upstream timeout after a report may have succeeded, and a query response too old for the card's freshness budget. Confirm that writes cannot double-apply, charts show stale state, and operators can recover the correlation key. Then test the silent case by withholding a scheduled heartbeat and checking that the external monitor notices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, write down the exit condition. Move to Datadog or another broad suite when integrated alerting and a deeper monitoring surface outweigh simplicity. Prefer Honeycomb or another tracing specialist when span-level investigation is mandatory. Reach for Grafana Cloud when managed ecosystem compatibility is the priority, or Prometheus when your team explicitly wants to own the metrics path. A hosted query API is a focused decision, not an observability strategy in disguise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If this boundary fits your system, start with the &lt;a href="https://api.infrai.cc/v1/discovery/flags.rollout" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;public Infrai discovery document&lt;/a&gt; and use its schema-driven examples to shape the server adapter.&lt;/p&gt;

