&lt;p&gt;An older account may contain useful correspondence or established contact information. However, these characteristics do not necessarily make it better than a newly created account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical usefulness of an email account depends on several factors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Communication Continuity&lt;br&gt;
An email address used over time may be familiar to friends, colleagues, and organizations. Maintaining secure access can help preserve existing communication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organized Correspondence&lt;br&gt;
Folders and archived messages can help users find previous conversations and maintain useful records.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Contact Management&lt;br&gt;
A well-maintained contact list can make everyday communication more convenient.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Account Security&lt;br&gt;
A properly protected account is more important than an account's creation date. Security settings and recovery information should be reviewed regularly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Legitimate Ownership&lt;br&gt;
Clear ownership helps prevent access disputes and ensures that private information remains under the appropriate person's control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security Risks of Accounts Obtained From Unknown Sources&lt;br&gt;
Obtaining an email account from an unfamiliar seller or third party can create problems that are difficult to predict.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Uncertain Ownership&lt;br&gt;
The original owner may still control recovery methods or other account-related information. This can make long-term access uncertain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recovery Difficulties&lt;br&gt;
If recovery details belong to another person, the current user may be unable to complete verification or restore access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Privacy Concerns&lt;br&gt;
An account may contain personal messages, documents, contacts, or notifications. Such information should remain protected from unauthorized access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unfamiliar Account Settings&lt;br&gt;
Previous settings may include forwarding rules, filters, connected applications, or other configurations that the new user does not understand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unexpected Security Verification&lt;br&gt;
Yahoo may request additional verification when it detects unusual activity or an unfamiliar sign-in environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Incomplete Account History&lt;br&gt;
Information provided by a third party may not reveal every previous account change or security concern.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These risks demonstrate why account age should never be treated as a guarantee of safety or reliability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yahoo Mail Security Best Practices&lt;br&gt;
Protecting an email account requires regular attention to authentication, recovery options, and account activity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a Strong, Unique Password&lt;br&gt;
Choose a password that is difficult to guess and is not reused on other services. A password manager can help maintain unique credentials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Enable Two-Step Verification&lt;br&gt;
Yahoo offers two-step verification to add another layer of protection to sign-ins. Use an available method that you can access reliably.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep Recovery Information Updated&lt;br&gt;
